window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"","h":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","p":"","t":"Method, decision gates and the operating sequence for a live Windows + Linux host investigation Commands are in the Execution Reference . Disk tool walkthroughs are in the FTK Runbook and Autopsy Runbook . Record everything in Hunt Notes . The theory behind collection, baselining and intel-driven hunting is in the Analyst Methods Handbook . Scenario assumed: one Windows 10 workstation and one Linux LAMP server, live access, Volatility / FTK / Autopsy on the bench, findings due at the end."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"part-1-the-method","h":"Part 1 — The method","p":"","t":"Methodology for live host analysis across a Windows workstation and a Linux server using Volatility, FTK, and Autopsy. The procedure is a fixed order of investigation; tools exist only to answer it."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"the-three-rules","h":"The three rules","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"Acquire before alter. Memory is captured from both hosts and hashed before any disk is touched. Every executed command modifies the state being examined, and volatile memory holds the evidence most likely to carry an active intrusion — running code, open sessions, injected regions, decrypted material. Disk-first handling destroys that evidence before it is read. Two independent sources or it is not confirmed. A single indicator is a lead. A finding requires two sources that agree and do not derive from each other: a process and its on-disk file, an event record and a matching artifact, a connection in memory and the same connection in a log. The term confirmed applies only when both are present. Everything else remains a lead until corroborated or discarded. State the reasoning. Each action is prefaced with the expected result and followed by what the output changed in the working picture. The method is the deliverable; output volume is not."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"the-five-questions","h":"The five questions","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"Host analysis answers five questions in fixed order. The order reflects evidence volatility: the first three are answered in memory, then proven on disk; the last two are answered on disk. The questions are platform-independent. Only the commands differ between Windows and Linux. Q1 What is running that should not be? Q2 What is talking that should not be? Q3 How does it survive a reboot? Q4 Who did it, and how did they get in? Q5 When did it start? The questions chain. A process identified in Q1 holds a connection in Q2, writes a file, registers persistence in Q3, executed under an account in Q4, and first appeared at a point in time in Q5. Each answer supplies the pivot for the next, and the chain reconstructs the intrusion end to end rather than producing isolated indicators. Memory answers Q1 through Q3 with higher fidelity than a live host, because a process cannot conceal its own memory pages from the kernel structures Volatility parses, whereas a tool running on the live host can be subverted by code already resident on it. Disk answers Q4 and Q5 and corroborates Q1 through Q3. Analysis proceeds from most volatile to least volatile."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"phase-0-orient","h":"Phase 0 — Orient","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"Record before collecting: hostname, IP, OS build, and privilege level on each host. Confirm what was provided — live shell, memory capture, disk image — and the tool versions on the bench. Identify the authorized output location; evidence media is never written to. Set the clock reference to UTC and open a findings log before the first command. State the plan: memory from both hosts, five questions on each, disk proof, no confirmation without a second source. This fixes the method before any result exists."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"phase-1-acquire","h":"Phase 1 — Acquire","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"Windows memory: winpmem or FTK Imager memory capture, hashed on output. Linux memory: AVML or LiME, hashed on output. If no Volatility symbol profile exists for the running Linux kernel, build an ISF from the kernel banner with dwarf2json so the image is parsable. Disk follows: acquire and hash-verify with FTK Imager, or mount read-only. All analysis runs against copies; originals are preserved and the acquisition hash is the integrity reference."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"phase-2-work-the-five-questions","h":"Phase 2 — Work the five\nquestions","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":""},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"q1-what-is-running-that-should-not-be","h":"Q1 — What is running\nthat should not be","p":"Part 1 — The method › Phase 2 — Work the five\nquestions","t":"Enumerate processes by two methods and compare. windows.pslist walks the active process list; windows.psscan scans pool memory for process objects. A process present in psscan but absent from pslist was unlinked from the active list and is a primary lead. Examine process lineage with windows.pstree and windows.cmdline ; parentage identifies anomalies faster than process names. High-signal lineage: Office or a browser spawning a command interpreter, a web service process spawning a shell, svchost.exe whose parent is not services.exe , lsass.exe whose parent is not wininit.exe . Run windows.malfind to locate private, committed, executable memory regions — the signature of injected or hollowed code. On the live host, flag binaries executing from user-writable paths and binaries with invalid or absent Authenticode signatures. An unexpected parent process is weighted above an unexpected process name. Linux memory equivalents: linux.pslist , linux.pstree , linux.malfind , and linux.check_modules for hidden kernel modules. On the live host, resolve each suspect PID to its backing file through /proc/<PID>/exe and read /proc/<PID>/cmdline ."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"q2-what-is-talking-that-should-not-be","h":"Q2 — What is talking\nthat should not be","p":"Part 1 — The method › Phase 2 — Work the five\nquestions","t":"Pull network state from the memory image with windows.netscan and linux.sockstat , not only from the live host, because live-host network utilities report only what the host is willing to disclose. Target two conditions: a process holding a session inconsistent with its function, and traffic at a fixed interval. Beaconing presents as a repeating small exchange at a regular period; normal client traffic is irregular. Every external address is carried forward — a single actor across two hosts commonly reuses infrastructure, so an address seen on the workstation is checked against the server. The DNS client cache and the hosts file expose pinned or injected name resolution."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"q3-how-does-it-survive-a-reboot","h":"Q3 — How does it survive a\nreboot","p":"Part 1 — The method › Phase 2 — Work the five\nquestions","t":"Persistence establishes intent. Execution alone can be incidental; a persistence mechanism is deliberate. Windows locations, highest yield first: services with image paths outside System32 , SysWOW64 , and Program Files ; scheduled tasks outside the \\Microsoft\\ tree; the Run and RunOnce keys under HKLM and HKCU; WMI __FilterToConsumerBinding subscriptions. The common pattern is an innocuous administrative name whose action points to a script in a data directory. Corroborate against windows.svcscan in memory. Linux locations: cron directories and user crontabs, enabled systemd unit files, shell profile scripts, /etc/ld.so.preload , and ~/.ssh/authorized_keys . A persistence entry whose target is the file identified in Q1 confirms the foothold."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"q4-who-did-it-and-how-did-they-get-in","h":"Q4 — Who did it, and how\ndid they get in","p":"Part 1 — The method › Phase 2 — Work the five\nquestions","t":"Shift from host to account. Windows logon events in the Security log distinguish local compromise from reach-in: type 2 interactive, type 3 network, type 10 remote interactive, event 4648 logon with explicit credentials, event 4672 assignment of administrative privileges. A network or explicit-credential logon from another host, or an account active outside its normal hours, indicates the workstation is not the origin and scope must extend outward. Enumerate local accounts and administrators group membership. Linux: /var/log/auth.log for accepted, failed, sudo, and session events; last and lastb for login records; any UID 0 account other than root. Where the entry vector was a web request rather than a credential, the auth records show no corresponding authentication, which is itself the finding."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"q5-when-did-it-start","h":"Q5 — When did it start","p":"Part 1 — The method › Phase 2 — Work the five\nquestions","t":"The first-appearance time is anchored in execution artifacts before any file timestamp is trusted, because file timestamps are the first thing altered. Windows: Prefetch write times, Amcache first-execution records, Shimcache, UserAssist, and BAM/DAM, cross-checked against $MFT and the USN journal. When standard-information timestamps appear uniform or implausible, compare them to the filename-attribute timestamps in the MFT record; disagreement indicates timestomping, and the execution artifact becomes the authoritative time source. Linux: build a filesystem timeline with fls and mactime and read it against the web and auth logs. The target time is the one preceding the alert that initiated the investigation; that interval is the portion of the intrusion not yet documented elsewhere."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"decision-gates","h":"Decision gates","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"A lead is promoted to a finding at a defined condition. G1 Process unsigned, in a writable path, or with an invalid parent -> acquire and hash the file G2 External session with no business justification -> bind it to a process and a log entry G3 Persistence entry targeting that same file or script -> foothold confirmed G4 The executing account and its authentication method -> extend scope to other hosts G5 A first-seen timestamp preceding the alert -> intrusion start identified Any gate that fires advances the chain to the next question. Breadth precedes depth: all five questions are cleared at a shallow level on both hosts before any single artifact is examined in depth, so the time budget is not consumed by one line of inquiry while the second host is unexamined."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"negative-findings","h":"Negative findings","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"Leads that fail to corroborate are documented, not discarded silently. A binary that appears suspicious but holds no connection, registers no persistence, and falls outside the established time window is recorded as unresolved after a bounded effort. An uncorroborated indicator is stated as uncorroborated. The distinction between a lead and a finding is the measured control."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"reporting","h":"Reporting","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"Each finding: plain text, UTC, hashed. FINDING: single line — what, where, first seen SCOPE: hosts - accounts - persistence and artifact locations - first/last seen UTC EVIDENCE: item - SHA-256 - source - collector and collection time corroborating source, independent of the first SCOPE BASIS: checks performed, and what evidence would change the conclusion No finding is reported as confirmed on a single source. Scope — which hosts, which accounts — is established before remediation is discussed."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"time-budget","h":"Time budget","p":"Part 1 — The method","t":"Scaled to a 3–4 hour window, both hosts: Orient and acquire (both hosts) 30 min ~15% Five questions, memory and live 80 min ~40% Disk proof and timeline 60 min ~30% Report and defend 30 min ~15% Record the lead, continue, return. No single artifact justifies consuming the budget at the cost of coverage."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"part-2-the-operating-sequence","h":"Part 2 — The operating\nsequence","p":"","t":"The organizing idea. Under time pressure the failure mode is flailing between tools. A fixed sequence removes the \"what next?\" decision. You are only ever running the next step and stating why. Run the same order on both hosts: VOLATILE -> LIVE -> DEAD memory running state image + artifacts (acquire) (triage) (Autopsy/FTK, timeline) | | | +---- record + hash as you go, corroborate ----+ | finding record"},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"pre-flight-before-the-clock-starts","h":"Pre-flight — before the\nclock starts","p":"Part 2 — The operating\nsequence","t":"Prove every tool works now, not when you are live on a box. A tool that fails under the clock is the most common avoidable loss. [ ] Volatility 3: `vol -h` runs; `vol -f <img> windows.info` works on a test image [ ] Vol2 fallback staged (version/profile mismatch is the usual reason you need it) [ ] Linux memory symbols: dwarf2json present; plan to build the ISF from the kernel banner (`banners.Banners`) if no profile is on hand [ ] FTK Imager launches (portable), can acquire + VERIFY [ ] Full FTK: database up, case opens (if provided) [ ] Autopsy opens; ingest modules present (Hash Lookup, Keyword, Recent Activity, Extension Mismatch) [ ] Memory acquisition ready: Windows -> winpmem or FTK Imager Capture Memory Linux -> AVML or LiME [ ] Case folder + evidence naming set; sha256 ready [ ] Hunt Notes open; both hosts added with their system time zones [ ] Execution Reference open State the method at the start: \"Volatile first, then live triage, then disk. I keep the footprint small, record and hash as I go, and I confirm every finding with a second independent source before I call it.\""},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"windows-10-sequence","h":"Windows 10 sequence","p":"Part 2 — The operating\nsequence","t":"MEMORY acquire: winpmem | FTK Imager -> Capture Memory (Exec Ref §3) vol3: windows.info, pslist/psscan/pstree, cmdline, netscan, malfind, svcscan, dlllist, handles, filescan, cmdscan/consoles (§4) hunt: unlinked procs (pslist vs psscan), odd parents, injected regions, rogue services, C2 in netscan LIVE Q1 procs §1.2 -> Q2 net §1.3 -> Q3 persistence §1.4 -> Q4 accounts §1.5 -> logs §1.7 -> Q5 execution artifacts §1.8 DISK FTK Imager acquire + VERIFY | mount read-only FTK Runbook or Autopsy Runbook, Q1 -> Q5 Q5 anchors: Prefetch, Amcache, UserAssist, BAM, $SI vs $FN"},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"linux-lamp-sequence","h":"Linux (LAMP) sequence","p":"Part 2 — The operating\nsequence","t":"MEMORY acquire: AVML | LiME (Exec Ref §3) vol3 + ISF: linux.pslist/pstree/psaux, bash, sockstat, malfind, check_modules/check_syscall, lsof (§5) LIVE Q1 procs §2.2 (www-data children!) -> Q2 net §2.3 -> Q3 persistence §2.4 -> Q4 accounts/auth §2.5 + web tier §2.7 + DB §2.8 -> Q5 file times §2.9 DISK image + Autopsy; fls + mactime timeline (§2.9) read against access.log and auth.log; .bash_history, package logs, /etc changes Each confirmed item = event + on-disk artifact (two independent sources), then a finding record."},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"time-discipline","h":"Time discipline","p":"Part 2 — The operating\nsequence","t":"[ ] Timebox each phase; check the clock at each host switch [ ] Note a lead, move on, come back. Never rabbit-hole live [ ] Record findings as you go. Do NOT leave reporting to the end [ ] Breadth across both hosts before depth on one artifact"},{"d":"Two-Host Investigation Runbook","s":"investigation-runbook","a":"tear-off","h":"Tear-off","p":"","t":"PRE-FLIGHT Vol3(+Vol2), dwarf2json, FTK Imager, FTK db, Autopsy, mem-acquire; sha256; Hunt Notes hosts+TZ APPROACH state method aloud; volatile -> live -> dead, same order on both hosts QUESTIONS Q1 running · Q2 talking · Q3 survives reboot · Q4 who/how in · Q5 when started GATES G1 hash it · G2 bind session to proc+log · G3 foothold · G4 extend scope · G5 start found WINDOWS mem(vol3) -> proc/net/persist/accounts/logs/exec-artifacts -> image + FTK/Autopsy -> timeline LINUX mem(ISF) -> proc/net/cron/systemd/auth/web/db -> image + Autopsy + fls/mactime REPORT finding / scope / evidence + 2nd source / scope basis. UTC, SHA-256 Defensive, authorized analysis on provided systems under rules of engagement. Acquire and hash before you alter."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"","h":"Execution Reference","p":"","t":"Host Triage · Memory · Forensic Tools · Detection Queries All runnable execution for a two-host investigation, organized by the five questions. Companion to the Analyst Methods Handbook and the Investigation Runbook. Scenario assumed: one Windows 10 workstation and one Linux LAMP server (Ubuntu likely). Tools: Vol3 (Vol2 as fallback), FTK Imager , FTK , Autopsy . All live commands are read-only. Run as admin/root. Write output to external media or a path you note, never over evidence. Section → question map. Every section answers one of the five questions. Tags like (Q3) in headings tell you which. Q Question Windows Linux Memory Disk Q1 What is running that should not be? §1.2 §2.2 §4, §5 §7D, §8B Q2 What is talking that should not be? §1.3 §2.3 §4, §5 logs Q3 How does it survive a reboot? §1.4 §2.4 svcscan, printkey §7B paths Q4 Who did it, and how did they get in? §1.5, §1.7 §2.5, §2.7 getsids logs Q5 When did it start? §1.6, §1.8 §2.6, §2.9 timeliner §7B, §8B Timeline Time rule: everything you record is UTC. Volatility prints UTC. PowerShell, Event Viewer, ls , Apache and syslog print the host's local time. Record each host's zone at orient (§1.1, §2.1) and convert in Hunt Notes or with the UTC forms below."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"0-drop-in-workflow","h":"0. Drop-In Workflow","p":"","t":"Order of operations. Memory first because every command you run changes it. Phase Goal Time (3–4 hr window) Section 0 Orient What's provided: live access, images, tool versions, where to write output, each host's time zone 10–15 min §0 1 Capture Memory first (both hosts), then disk image if required 20–30 min §3, §7A 2 Live triage Q1 → Q5 shallow pass: procs → network → persistence → accounts → logs 30 min/host §1, §2 3 Memory Confirm/expand live findings, find hidden activity 40–45 min §4–§6 4 Disk Prove it on disk: files, execution artifacts, timestamps, logs 50–60 min §7 (Imager, FTK), §8 (Autopsy) 5 Report Findings with evidence + second source 20 min §9 Breadth before depth: clear Q1–Q5 shallow on both hosts before going deep on any one artifact. Record the lead, continue, come back. Orient checklist [ ] Hostname / IP / OS build / system time zone recorded for each box [ ] Am I admin/root? (whoami /groups | findstr High / id) [ ] Tool versions: vol -h | head -1 · FTK Imager / FTK Help>About · Autopsy Help>About [ ] FTK: database service up, login works [ ] Output location chosen and noted [ ] Hunt Notes open, hosts added with their time zones (§9) The pivot loop. Every lead runs the same circle until it closes (Q1 → Q2 → Q3 → Q4 → Q5): suspicious PROCESS → PID / PPID / cmdline → NETWORK (who it talks to) → FILE on disk (path, hash, timestamps) → PERSISTENCE (how it survives) → ACCOUNT (who ran it, how they got in) → TIMELINE (first seen) → back to PROCESS"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-win10-live-triage-powershell-admin","h":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","p":"","t":""},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-1-context","h":"1.1 Context","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"hostname; whoami /all; (Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem) | Select Caption,Version,BuildNumber,LastBootUpTime Get-Date -Format o; Get-TimeZone; (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().ToString('u') Get-Date -Format o shows the offset (e.g. -10:00 ). Put that zone on the host in Hunt Notes."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-2-processes-q1","h":"1.2 Processes (Q1)","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | Select ProcessId,ParentProcessId,Name,@{n='CreatedUTC';e={$_.CreationDate.ToUniversalTime().ToString('u')}},ExecutablePath,CommandLine | Sort CreatedUTC | Format-Table -AutoSize -Wrap Running from user-writable paths: Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | ? { $_.ExecutablePath -match '\\\\(Temp|AppData|Users\\\\Public|ProgramData|Downloads)\\\\' } | Select ProcessId,ParentProcessId,Name,ExecutablePath,CommandLine Unsigned running binaries: Get-Process | ? Path | % { $s = Get-AuthenticodeSignature $_.Path; if ($s.Status -ne 'Valid') { [pscustomobject]@{PID=$_.Id;Name=$_.Name;Path=$_.Path;Sig=$s.Status} } } Parent of a PID: $p=<PID>; Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter \"ProcessId=$p\" | Select Name,ParentProcessId,CommandLine Red flags: winword/excel/outlook → cmd/powershell , w3wp/httpd → cmd , powershell -enc , rundll32 with no DLL args, svchost not parented by services.exe , lsass with a parent other than wininit ."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-3-network-q2","h":"1.3 Network (Q2)","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"Get-NetTCPConnection -State Established,Listen | Select LocalAddress,LocalPort,RemoteAddress,RemotePort,State,OwningProcess,@{n='Proc';e={(Get-Process -Id $_.OwningProcess -EA 0).Name}} | Sort State,RemoteAddress | Format-Table -AutoSize netstat -anob Get-DnsClientCache | Select Entry,Data | Sort Entry Get-Content C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\etc\\hosts | ? { $_ -notmatch '^\\s*#' -and $_.Trim() }"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-4-persistence-q3","h":"1.4 Persistence (Q3)","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"Run keys: 'HKLM:\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run','HKLM:\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\RunOnce','HKCU:\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run','HKCU:\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\RunOnce','HKLM:\\Software\\WOW6432Node\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run' | % { if (Test-Path $_) { \"== $_\"; Get-ItemProperty $_ | Select * -Exclude PS* | Format-List } } Services outside normal paths: Get-CimInstance Win32_Service | ? { $_.PathName -notmatch 'System32|SysWOW64|Program Files' } | Select Name,State,StartMode,StartName,PathName Scheduled tasks (non-Microsoft): Get-ScheduledTask | ? TaskPath -notlike '\\Microsoft\\*' | Select TaskName,TaskPath,State,@{n='Run';e={($_.Actions | % { \"$($_.Execute) $($_.Arguments)\" }) -join '; '}} | Format-Table -AutoSize -Wrap WMI event subscriptions: Get-CimInstance -Namespace root\\subscription -ClassName __EventFilter; Get-CimInstance -Namespace root\\subscription -ClassName CommandLineEventConsumer; Get-CimInstance -Namespace root\\subscription -ClassName __FilterToConsumerBinding Startup folders: Get-ChildItem \"C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\StartUp\",\"C:\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\Startup\" -Force -EA 0 Defender exclusions (attackers add these): Get-MpPreference | Select ExclusionPath,ExclusionProcess,ExclusionExtension Service DLLs outside System32 (svchost-hosted services hide here; the path filter above misses them): Get-ChildItem HKLM:\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services -EA 0 | % { $d=(Get-ItemProperty \"$($_.PSPath)\\Parameters\" -EA 0).ServiceDll; if ($d -and $d -notmatch '\\\\System32\\\\') { [pscustomobject]@{Service=$_.PSChildName;ServiceDll=$d} } } Winlogon, IFEO debugger hijacks, BITS jobs: Get-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon' | Select Shell,Userinit Get-ChildItem 'HKLM:\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Image File Execution Options' -EA 0 | % { $g=(Get-ItemProperty $_.PSPath -EA 0).Debugger; if ($g) { \"$($_.PSChildName) -> $g\" } } Get-BitsTransfer -AllUsers -EA 0 | Select JobId,DisplayName,TransferType,JobState,@{n='Files';e={$_.FileList.RemoteName -join '; '}} Expected: Shell = explorer.exe , Userinit = C:\\Windows\\system32\\userinit.exe, (trailing comma is normal). Anything appended is persistence."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-5-accounts-q4","h":"1.5 Accounts (Q4)","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"Get-LocalUser | Select Name,Enabled,LastLogon,PasswordLastSet; Get-LocalGroupMember Administrators quser 2>$null; Get-SmbShare; net session 2>$null"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-6-files-q1-q5","h":"1.6 Files (Q1 / Q5)","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"Recently written files in common staging paths: Get-ChildItem C:\\Users\\*\\AppData,C:\\Users\\Public,C:\\ProgramData,C:\\Windows\\Temp -Recurse -File -Force -EA 0 | ? LastWriteTimeUtc -gt (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().AddDays(-7) | Sort LastWriteTimeUtc -Desc | Select -First 50 LastWriteTimeUtc,Length,FullName Prefetch (what ran recently; full method in §1.8): Get-ChildItem C:\\Windows\\Prefetch -EA 0 | Sort LastWriteTimeUtc -Desc | Select -First 30 CreationTimeUtc,LastWriteTimeUtc,Name PowerShell history (all users): Get-ChildItem C:\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadLine\\ConsoleHost_history.txt -EA 0 | % { \"== $($_.FullName)\"; Get-Content $_ } Hash a file: Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 '<path>'"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-7-event-logs-q4-q5","h":"1.7 Event Logs (Q4 / Q5)","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"TimeCreated is local time. The queries below add a UTC column; use it. Log ID Meaning Security 4624 / 4625 Logon success / fail (check LogonType: 2 interactive, 3 network, 10 RDP) Security 4648 Logon with explicit credentials (runas, lateral movement) Security 4672 Admin-equivalent logon Security 4688 Process create (only if auditing is on) Security 4720 / 4732 User created / added to group Security 4697 / 4698 / 4702 Service installed / scheduled task created / task updated Security 1102 Security log cleared System 7045 Service installed System 104 Log cleared PowerShell/Operational 4104 Script block Defender/Operational 1116 / 1117 Detection / action Sysmon/Operational 1, 3, 7, 8, 10, 11, 13, 22 Proc, net, image load, remote thread, process access (LSASS), file create, reg set, DNS Logons with user, type and source IP: Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security';Id=4624} -MaxEvents 500 | % { [pscustomobject]@{UTC=$_.TimeCreated.ToUniversalTime().ToString('u');User=$_.Properties[5].Value;Type=$_.Properties[8].Value;IP=$_.Properties[18].Value;Proc=$_.Properties[17].Value} } | ? { $_.Type -in 3,10 } | Format-Table -AutoSize Failed logons by source: Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security';Id=4625} -EA 0 | % { $_.Properties[19].Value } | Group | Sort Count -Desc | Select Count,Name Service installs, task creation, log clears: Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System';Id=7045,104} -EA 0 | Select @{n='UTC';e={$_.TimeCreated.ToUniversalTime().ToString('u')}},Id,Message | Format-List Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security';Id=4648,4697,4698,4720,4732,1102} -EA 0 | Select @{n='UTC';e={$_.TimeCreated.ToUniversalTime().ToString('u')}},Id,Message | Format-List PowerShell script blocks with common bad terms: Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational';Id=4104} -EA 0 | ? { $_.Message -match 'FromBase64String|IEX|Invoke-Expression|DownloadString|Net.WebClient|-enc' } | Select @{n='UTC';e={$_.TimeCreated.ToUniversalTime().ToString('u')}},@{n='Script';e={$_.Message.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(400,$_.Message.Length))}} | Format-List Sysmon (if installed): Get-WinEvent -LogName 'Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational' -MaxEvents 300 -EA 0 | ? Id -in 1,3,10,22 | Select @{n='UTC';e={$_.TimeCreated.ToUniversalTime().ToString('u')}},Id,Message | Format-List"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"1-8-execution-artifacts-native-q5","h":"1.8 Execution Artifacts,\nNative (Q5)","p":"1. Win10 Live Triage\n(PowerShell, admin)","t":"When did it first run? Anchor start time on execution artifacts before trusting file timestamps; file timestamps are the first thing an attacker alters. No Zimmerman tools needed. Every output below is already UTC, so it goes straight into Hunt Notes with zone UTC . Artifact Proves Time it gives Native read Prefetch C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\NAME-HASH.pf Program executed .pf created ≈ first run · modified ≈ last run (last 8 run times are inside the file, needs a parser) below UserAssist (NTUSER) GUI launch by that user Last run + run count below (ROT13 decode) BAM/DAM (SYSTEM) Executed, per user SID (Win10 1709+) Last run below Amcache C:\\Windows\\appcompat\\Programs\\Amcache.hve Present / executed, with SHA-1 Key last-write ≈ first seen locked live: export with FTK Imager, view in Registry Viewer or Autopsy Shimcache / AppCompatCache (SYSTEM) File existed (execution not guaranteed on Win10) File's own modified time vol windows.shimcachemem (newer Vol3) or Vol2 shimcache $MFT SI vs FN times Timestomping $FILE_NAME times are hard to fake Autopsy → File Metadata tab (istat shows both), FTK Imager export Prefetch, first and last run: Get-ChildItem C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\*.pf -EA 0 | Select Name,@{n='FirstRunUTC';e={$_.CreationTimeUtc.ToString('u')}},@{n='LastRunUTC';e={$_.LastWriteTimeUtc.ToString('u')}} | Sort FirstRunUTC -Desc | Select -First 40 UserAssist, decoded (run per user; for another user's hive, export NTUSER.DAT and read offline): Get-ChildItem 'HKCU:\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\UserAssist\\*\\Count' -EA 0 | % { $k=$_; $k.GetValueNames() | % { $v=$_; $n=-join ($v.ToCharArray() | % { if ($_ -match '[A-Za-z]') { $b=if ([char]::IsUpper($_)) {65} else {97}; [char]((([int]$_ - $b + 13) % 26) + $b) } else { $_ } }) $d=$k.GetValue($v); if ($d.Length -ge 68) { [pscustomobject]@{Program=$n;Runs=[BitConverter]::ToInt32($d,4);LastRunUTC=[DateTime]::FromFileTimeUtc([BitConverter]::ToInt64($d,60)).ToString('u')} } } } | ? Runs -gt 0 | Sort LastRunUTC -Desc BAM, last run per user (Win10 1809+ path first, older path second): 'HKLM:\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\bam\\State\\UserSettings','HKLM:\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\bam\\UserSettings' | ? { Test-Path $_ } | % { Get-ChildItem $_ } | % { $k=$_; $k.GetValueNames() | ? { $_ -like '\\Device\\*' } | % { [pscustomobject]@{SID=$k.PSChildName;Path=$_;LastRunUTC=[DateTime]::FromFileTimeUtc([BitConverter]::ToInt64($k.GetValue($_),0)).ToString('u')} } } | Sort LastRunUTC -Desc Resolve a SID: (New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier '<SID>').Translate([System.Security.Principal.NTAccount]) Timestomp check (disk). In Autopsy, select the file → File Metadata tab. Compare $STANDARD_INFORMATION against $FILE_NAME times. SI earlier than FN, or SI with .0000000 sub-seconds, points to timestomping. Use the FN time and the execution artifacts as the start-time source. To timeline. One line per artifact, with that artifact's time, source = the artifact ( prefetch , userassist , bam , amcache , $MFT FN ), Q5. Two artifacts that agree on first run = a confirmed start time."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-linux-live-triage-root","h":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","p":"","t":""},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-1-context","h":"2.1 Context","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"hostname; uname -a; cat /etc/os-release | head -3; uptime; date; date -u; id timedatectl 2>/dev/null | grep -i 'time zone' || cat /etc/timezone Put that zone on the host in Hunt Notes. Apache logs carry their own offset; syslog/auth.log lines do not. who -a; last -Faiw | head -30; lastb -Faiw 2>/dev/null | head -20"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-2-processes-q1","h":"2.2 Processes (Q1)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"ps -eo pid,ppid,user,lstart,etime,cmd --forest Binaries deleted from disk or running from temp: ls -l /proc/[0-9]*/exe 2>/dev/null | grep -E '\\(deleted\\)|/tmp/|/dev/shm/|/var/tmp/' What is the web server spawning? (Shells under apache/www-data = webshell.) ps -u www-data -f; pstree -apl $(pgrep -o apache2 || pgrep -o httpd) Deep-dive one PID: P=<PID>; ls -l /proc/$P/exe /proc/$P/cwd; tr '\\0' ' ' < /proc/$P/cmdline; echo; tr '\\0' '\\n' < /proc/$P/environ | head; ls -l /proc/$P/fd | head -30"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-3-network-q2","h":"2.3 Network (Q2)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"ss -tulpn; ss -tanp state established lsof -i -nP 2>/dev/null | grep -v LISTEN cat /etc/hosts; cat /etc/resolv.conf"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-4-persistence-q3","h":"2.4 Persistence (Q3)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"Cron, all users and system: for u in $(cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd); do crontab -l -u \"$u\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^#' | sed \"s/^/[$u] /\"; done; ls -la /etc/cron* /var/spool/cron/crontabs 2>/dev/null; cat /etc/crontab systemd: enabled units (persistence that is stopped right now still survives reboot), then running, then timers: systemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled --no-pager systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running --no-pager; systemctl list-timers --all --no-pager Recently modified unit files: find /etc/systemd /lib/systemd /usr/lib/systemd /home/*/.config/systemd /root/.config/systemd -name '*.service' -mtime -14 -ls 2>/dev/null SSH keys, shell rc files, preload (rootkit tell): find / -xdev -name authorized_keys -exec ls -la {} \\; -exec cat {} \\; 2>/dev/null ls -la /etc/ld.so.preload 2>/dev/null && cat /etc/ld.so.preload ls -la /etc/rc.local /etc/profile.d/ 2>/dev/null; tail -5 /root/.bashrc /home/*/.bashrc Other survivors: at jobs, SysV init, MOTD scripts, PAM hooks, file capabilities: atq 2>/dev/null; ls -la /etc/init.d/ /etc/update-motd.d/ 2>/dev/null grep -rnE 'pam_exec|pam_script' /etc/pam.d/ 2>/dev/null getcap -r / 2>/dev/null"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-5-accounts-q4","h":"2.5 Accounts (Q4)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"awk -F: '$3==0 {print \"UID0:\",$1}' /etc/passwd; awk -F: '$7 !~ /(nologin|false)$/ {print $1,$3,$6,$7}' /etc/passwd getent group sudo wheel; grep -vE '^\\s*(#|$)' /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/* 2>/dev/null; ls -la --time-style=full-iso /etc/passwd /etc/shadow /etc/group Auth log (Ubuntu auth.log , RHEL secure ): grep -hE 'Accepted|Failed password|Invalid user|sudo:.*COMMAND|useradd|usermod' /var/log/auth.log* /var/log/secure* 2>/dev/null | tail -100 grep -h 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log* 2>/dev/null | grep -oE 'from [0-9.]+' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head No auth.log (journald-only systems). --utc prints UTC: journalctl --utc -o short-iso _COMM=sshd --no-pager | tail -100 journalctl --utc -o short-iso _COMM=sudo --no-pager | tail -50 Shell history, all users: for h in /root/.*_history /home/*/.*_history; do [ -f \"$h\" ] && { echo \"== $h\"; cat \"$h\"; }; done"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-6-files-q1-q5","h":"2.6 Files (Q1 / Q5)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"ls -la /tmp /var/tmp /dev/shm; find /tmp /var/tmp /dev/shm -type f -exec file {} \\; 2>/dev/null Modified in the last 3 days (adjust): find / -xdev -type f -mtime -3 ! -path '/proc/*' ! -path '/sys/*' ! -path '/run/*' ! -path '/var/lib/*' 2>/dev/null | head -200 Between two times: find / -xdev -type f -newermt '2026-10-19 00:00' ! -newermt '2026-10-21 00:00' 2>/dev/null | head -200 SUID binaries (compare against a clean box): find / -xdev -perm -4000 -type f -exec ls -la {} \\; 2>/dev/null Package integrity (changed system binaries): dpkg --verify 2>/dev/null | grep -v ' c ' | head -50 # Debian/Ubuntu rpm -Va 2>/dev/null | grep -E '^..5' | head -50 # RHEL Kernel modules / taint: lsmod; cat /proc/sys/kernel/tainted; dmesg 2>/dev/null | grep -iE 'taint|module verification' | tail"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-7-web-tier-apache-php-q1-q4","h":"2.7 Web Tier, Apache / PHP (Q1 /\nQ4)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"Webroot location: grep -RhiE '^\\s*DocumentRoot' /etc/apache2 /etc/httpd 2>/dev/null Recent PHP and webshell patterns: find /var/www -type f -name '*.php*' -mtime -30 -ls 2>/dev/null grep -RnliE 'eval\\(|base64_decode|gzinflate|str_rot13|shell_exec|passthru|system\\(|proc_open|assert\\(|\\$_(GET|POST|REQUEST|COOKIE)\\[' /var/www --include='*.php*' 2>/dev/null Files in webroot not owned by the expected user: find /var/www ! -user www-data ! -user root -ls 2>/dev/null | head Access log (Ubuntu /var/log/apache2/ , RHEL /var/log/httpd/ ): L=/var/log/apache2/access.log awk '{print $1}' $L | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head # top client IPs awk '$6 ~ /POST/ {print $7}' $L | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head # POST targets grep -iE 'cmd=|exec=|c=|union.*select|\\.\\./|/etc/passwd|wget|curl|base64|%3Cscript' $L | tail -50 awk -F'\"' '{print $6}' $L | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head # user agents awk '{print $9}' $L | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn # status codes First and last hit for an attacker IP: grep -h '<IP>' /var/log/apache2/access.log.1 /var/log/apache2/access.log 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1p;$p' PHP errors often show the upload or exploit: tail -100 /var/log/apache2/error.log"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-8-database-mysql-mariadb-q3-q4","h":"2.8 Database, MySQL / MariaDB\n(Q3 / Q4)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"mysql -u root -p -e \"SELECT user,host,plugin FROM mysql.user; SELECT * FROM mysql.func; SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'secure_file_priv'; SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'general_log%';\" Any row in mysql.func is a user-defined function. A UDF loaded from the plugin dir is a known way to get OS command execution from the DB. ls -la /var/log/mysql/; grep -iE 'into outfile|into dumpfile|load_file|grant all' /var/log/mysql/*.log 2>/dev/null | tail"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"2-9-file-times-and-timeline-q5","h":"2.9 File Times and Timeline\n(Q5)","p":"2. Linux Live Triage (root)","t":"ls / find print local time. Use these forms for UTC and birth time: TZ=UTC stat -c '%n | birth %w | mod %y | change %z | access %x' <file> TZ=UTC find /var/www /tmp /var/tmp /dev/shm -type f -newermt '2026-10-19 00:00' -printf '%TY-%Tm-%Td %TT %p\\n' 2>/dev/null | sort birth - means the kernel/coreutils can't report it. On ext4, read the inode's crtime (debugfs opens read-only by default): debugfs -R \"stat <$(stat -c %i <file>)>\" \"$(df --output=source <file> | tail -1)\" 2>/dev/null | grep -E 'crtime|mtime|ctime' ctime newer than mtime means the mtime was set back ( touch -d ), which is the Linux timestomp tell. Filesystem timeline from an image (Sleuth Kit, on the analysis box): mmls image.dd # find the partition offset (sectors) fls -r -m / -o <OFFSET> image.dd > body.txt mactime -b body.txt -d -z UTC 2026-10-19..2026-10-22 > fs-timeline.csv Read fs-timeline.csv against access.log and auth.log around the first suspicious hit."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"3-memory-capture-setup","h":"3. Memory Capture (Setup)","p":"","t":"Win10 — FTK Imager (run from USB): File → Capture Memory → destination on external/output path → filename HOST_mem.mem → optional Include pagefile → Capture. Hash it after. Alternative if winpmem is provided: .\\winpmem_mini_x64.exe E:\\HOST_mem.raw Linux — AVML (static binary, no build needed, preferred): sudo ./avml /mnt/usb/HOST_mem.lime && sha256sum /mnt/usb/HOST_mem.lime Linux — LiME (module must match the running kernel): sudo insmod lime-$(uname -r).ko \"path=/mnt/usb/HOST_mem.lime format=lime\" Record the kernel. You need it for symbols: uname -r; cat /proc/version"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"4-volatility-3-windows","h":"4. Volatility 3 — Windows","p":"","t":"Invocation is vol (pip) or python3 vol.py (git). Add -r csv > out.csv to save, -o <dir> for dumps. Volatility timestamps are UTC. vol -f mem.raw windows.info Step Plugin Why Process list windows.pslist · windows.pstree Baseline and parentage Hidden / exited windows.psscan In psscan but not pslist = hidden or terminated Command lines windows.cmdline · windows.cmdscan · windows.consoles (newer Vol3) Arguments, encoded PowerShell, typed console commands Network windows.netscan Connections → PID Injection windows.malfind RWX private memory with PE/shellcode Modules windows.dlllist --pid N · windows.ldrmodules --pid N Odd paths; unlinked DLLs (False in lists) Services windows.svcscan Odd binaries / names Handles windows.handles --pid N Files, mutexes, registry keys Persistence windows.registry.printkey --key \"Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\" Run keys (needs hive offset for NTUSER) Accounts windows.getsids --pid N · windows.hashdump Who ran it Files windows.filescan Find file objects, then dump Kernel / rootkit windows.ssdt · windows.callbacks · windows.driverscan · windows.modscan Hooks, unknown drivers Execution history windows.shimcachemem (newer Vol3) Shimcache entries from memory (Q5) Timeline timeliner.Timeliner All timestamped artifacts Typical run: vol -f mem.raw windows.pstree vol -f mem.raw windows.psscan > psscan.txt; vol -f mem.raw windows.pslist > pslist.txt # diff PIDs vol -f mem.raw windows.cmdline vol -f mem.raw windows.netscan vol -f mem.raw windows.malfind Pivot on a PID: vol -f mem.raw windows.dlllist --pid <PID> vol -f mem.raw windows.handles --pid <PID> vol -f mem.raw -o dump/ windows.pslist --pid <PID> --dump vol -f mem.raw -o dump/ windows.malfind --pid <PID> --dump Find and dump a file: vol -f mem.raw windows.filescan | grep -i '<name>' vol -f mem.raw -o dump/ windows.dumpfiles --virtaddr <OFFSET> Registry hives, then a key: vol -f mem.raw windows.registry.hivelist vol -f mem.raw windows.registry.printkey --offset <HIVE_OFFSET> --key \"Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\" List plugin names in this build (they shift between versions): vol -h | grep -E '^\\s+windows\\.'"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"5-volatility-3-linux","h":"5. Volatility 3 — Linux","p":"","t":"Symbols are the blocker. Vol3 Linux plugins need an ISF JSON that matches the exact kernel. Get the banner: vol -f mem.lime banners.Banners Check whether the symbols are already present: vol isfinfo.IsfInfo 2>/dev/null | grep -i linux; ls volatility3/symbols/linux/ 2>/dev/null If missing, build on a box with the matching kernel debug image (Ubuntu: linux-image-$(uname -r)-dbgsym from ddebs.ubuntu.com): ./dwarf2json linux --elf /usr/lib/debug/boot/vmlinux-<KVER> --system-map /boot/System.map-<KVER> > ubuntu-<KVER>.json cp ubuntu-<KVER>.json <volatility3>/volatility3/symbols/linux/ Step Plugin Why Processes linux.pslist · linux.pstree · linux.psaux List, tree, full args Shell history linux.bash Commands typed, even if history file was wiped Network linux.sockstat (older: linux.netstat ) Sockets → PID Open files linux.lsof --pid N Files, sockets per PID Injection linux.malfind RWX regions Env linux.envars --pid N LD_PRELOAD, odd PATH Maps linux.proc.Maps --pid N Mapped libs, anonymous exec regions Rootkit linux.check_syscall · linux.check_modules · linux.lsmod · linux.hidden_modules · linux.tty_check · linux.check_afinfo Hooked syscalls, hidden LKMs vol -f mem.lime linux.pstree vol -f mem.lime linux.psaux vol -f mem.lime linux.bash vol -f mem.lime linux.sockstat vol -f mem.lime linux.malfind vol -f mem.lime linux.check_syscall; vol -f mem.lime linux.check_modules Plugin names in this build: vol -h | grep -E '^\\s+linux\\.'"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"6-volatility-2-fallback","h":"6. Volatility 2 Fallback","p":"","t":"Use when Vol3 errors on the image, symbols are missing and a Vol2 profile is available, or you want a plugin your Vol3 build lacks ( psxview , mftparser , shimcache ; recent Vol3 releases added cmdscan / consoles . Check vol -h ). Vol2 support stops around 2019–2020 Win10 builds. A newer build may not have a profile; stay in Vol3 then."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"6-1-windows","h":"6.1 Windows","p":"6. Volatility 2 Fallback","t":"Profile: vol.py -f mem.raw imageinfo # slow; or: vol.py -f mem.raw kdbgscan vol.py --info | grep Win10 # pick closest Win10x64_<build> Set the profile once: export VOLATILITY_PROFILE=Win10x64_<BUILD>; export VOLATILITY_LOCATION=file://$(pwd)/mem.raw Vol3 Vol2 equivalent windows.pslist / pstree / psscan pslist · pstree · psscan · psxview (cross-view hidden procs) windows.cmdline cmdline · cmdscan · consoles (typed commands + output) windows.netscan netscan windows.malfind malfind -D out/ windows.dlllist / ldrmodules dlllist -p N · ldrmodules -p N windows.handles handles -p N -t File,Mutant,Key windows.svcscan svcscan registry.printkey printkey -K \"Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\" windows.hashdump hashdump windows.filescan / dumpfiles filescan · dumpfiles -Q <offset> -D out/ pslist --dump procdump -p N -D out/ ssdt / driverscan / modscan ssdt · driverirp · modscan · apihooks timeliner timeliner · mftparser · shimcache · userassist vol.py psxview; vol.py cmdscan; vol.py consoles; vol.py netscan; vol.py malfind -D out/"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"6-2-linux","h":"6.2 Linux","p":"6. Volatility 2 Fallback","t":"Needs a profile zip built against the target kernel: cd volatility/tools/linux && make && zip Ubuntu_<KVER>.zip module.dwarf /boot/System.map-<KVER> cp Ubuntu_<KVER>.zip ../../volatility/plugins/overlays/linux/ vol.py --info | grep Linux # get profile name, e.g. LinuxUbuntu_<KVER>x64 vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_pstree vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_psaux vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_bash vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_netstat vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_lsof -p <PID> vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_malfind vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_check_syscall; vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_hidden_modules vol.py --profile=<P> -f mem.lime linux_enumerate_files | grep -E '/tmp|/dev/shm'"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"7-ftk-imager","h":"7. FTK Imager","p":"","t":"FTK Imager is acquisition and preview. It is not an analysis suite. Use it to capture, browse, export and hash, then analyze in Autopsy, FTK, or Vol. Full FTK (Forensic Toolkit) is covered in §7C–§7D. Expect to use Imager to produce the image and FTK to analyze it."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"7a-setup-capture-load-verify","h":"7A. Setup (capture, load,\nverify)","p":"7. FTK Imager","t":"Capture memory (live Win10): File → Capture Memory → set destination and filename → Include pagefile (optional) → Capture Memory. Image a disk: File → Create Disk Image Source: Physical Drive (whole disk), Logical Drive (one volume), Image File (convert), or Contents of a Folder Add destination → type E01 (compressed, carries hashes) or Raw (dd) Fill evidence info (case, examiner, notes) Check Verify images after they are created → Start Keep the generated .txt log. It holds the MD5/SHA1. Grab locked registry/system files from a live box: File → Obtain Protected Files → destination → Password recovery and all registry files . Load evidence to browse: File → Add Evidence Item → Physical Drive / Logical Drive / Image File / Contents of a Folder → select. Mount an image read-only (so other tools can read it): File → Image Mounting → image file → Mount Type Physical & Logical → Mount Method Block Device / Read Only → Mount. It shows up as a drive letter. Hash verify an existing image: right-click the evidence item → Verify Drive/Image ."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"7b-hunt-navigation","h":"7B. Hunt Navigation","p":"7. FTK Imager","t":"Screen layout. Evidence Tree (top-left) → File List (top-right) → Viewer (bottom: Text / Hex / Auto). Properties pane on the left. Red X on an icon = deleted file (still recoverable if not overwritten) Click a column header in File List to sort. Sort Date Modified descending to see recent activity. Right-click → Export Files (copy out for hashing or other tools) or Export File Hash List View → Hex to inspect headers ( MZ = PE executable, 7F 45 4C 46 = ELF) Where to go — Windows image ( [root] under the NTFS partition): Path What it tells you $MFT (root) Every file record. Export for MFTECmd/Autopsy Windows\\System32\\config\\ SAM, SYSTEM, SOFTWARE, SECURITY hives Users\\<u>\\NTUSER.DAT · AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Windows\\UsrClass.dat User hive: Run keys, RecentDocs, ShellBags Windows\\System32\\winevt\\Logs\\ EVTX: Security, System, PowerShell/Operational, Sysmon Windows\\Prefetch\\ Execution evidence ( NAME-HASH.pf ) Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\ Scheduled task XML ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\StartUp\\ All-users startup Users\\<u>\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Recent\\ LNK files: what was opened Users\\<u>\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadLine\\ PS history Users\\<u>\\Downloads , AppData\\Local\\Temp , Users\\Public Staging locations $Recycle.Bin\\<SID>\\ $I (metadata) and $R (content) pairs inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles\\ IIS logs, if present Where to go — Linux image (ext4 partition): Path What it tells you /var/log/auth.log* · syslog* · apache2/ · mysql/ Logons, sudo, web hits, DB /var/www/ Webroot: webshells, modified PHP /etc/passwd · shadow · group · sudoers.d/ Accounts and privilege /etc/cron* · /var/spool/cron/crontabs/ Cron persistence /etc/systemd/system/ · /lib/systemd/system/ Service persistence /root/ · /home/*/ → .bash_history , .ssh/authorized_keys , .bashrc User activity and backdoors /tmp/ · /var/tmp/ · /dev/shm/ (if captured) Dropped tools /etc/ld.so.preload Userland rootkit"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"7c-ftk-forensic-toolkit-setup","h":"7C. FTK (Forensic Toolkit) —\nSetup","p":"7. FTK Imager","t":"FTK is a case-based suite backed by a database. Processing (hashing, indexing, carving) runs up front, then you search and filter. Menu names shift between FTK 7 and 8; the flow below holds. Before anything: confirm the database service is running and you can log in. FTK will not open a case without it. Credentials are usually set by whoever built the box. Ask, don't guess. New case: File → New Case (or Case Manager → New ) Case name CASE_<host> , description, case folder on the output drive Processing profile: pick Forensics or a custom profile. Avoid Field Mode , which skips the indexing you'll search on. Click Customize on the profile and set: Turn on Turn off (time sinks) MD5 + SHA-1 hashes Explicit Image Detection Flag Bad Extensions Optical Character Recognition Expand Compound Files Data Carving (run later if needed) dtSearch Text Index Thumbnails for videos KFF (Known File Filter), if a KFF library is configured Cerberus malware triage, unless you want it and time allows Registry Reports Create Thumbnails for Graphics (optional) OK → Add Evidence dialog opens Add evidence ( Evidence → Add/Remove later): Acquired Image of Drive — E01 / raw from FTK Imager (most common) All Images in Directory — multiple images at once Contents of a Folder / Individual File — exported artifacts, memory-dump outputs Physical Drive / Logical Drive — attached media Set time zone = the target's . Wrong time zone corrupts every timestamp you report. Convert what you record to UTC (Hunt Notes, zone = that host). Processing: watch the Progress window. You can start browsing as categories populate, but Index Search results are incomplete until indexing finishes. Memory images: some FTK builds accept a memory dump as evidence and parse processes and sockets. Don't rely on it. Do memory in Vol3/Vol2 (§4–§6) and add dumped files back as Individual File evidence. Add more processing later: Evidence → Additional Analysis (carving, more hashing, KFF) without rebuilding the case."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"7d-ftk-hunt-navigation","h":"7D. FTK — Hunt Navigation","p":"7. FTK Imager","t":"Tabs across the top. Work them in this order. Tab Use it for Overview Start here. Counts by File Status (Bad Extension, Deleted, Encrypted, KFF Alert , KFF Ignorable) and File Category (Executable, Documents, Archives) Explore File-system tree, same as FTK Imager. Use for the path tables in §7B Index Search Fast keyword search over the dtSearch index (IP, filename, eval( , base64_decode ) Live Search Hex/text pattern search (slower, no index needed). Use for hex signatures Bookmarks Your marked evidence. Feeds the report Graphics · Email Only if relevant First moves: Overview → KFF Alert. Known-bad hash hits. Fastest win if a KFF library is loaded. Overview → Bad Extension. Renamed executables ( .jpg that's a PE). Overview → File Category → Executable. Sort by Created/Modified. Look at anything in user, Temp, or ProgramData paths. Overview → Deleted Files. Tools removed after use. Index Search for indicators from live triage and Vol (IPs, filenames, PIDs' image names). Explore the §7B paths for persistence and logs. File List controls: Filter dropdown (toolbar). Start with Unfiltered , then try built-ins like Actual Files to drop slack and free space. Make your own via Filter Manager (e.g. Modified > date AND Category = Executable). Column settings (right-click header). Add Created / Modified / Accessed, Path, MD5, Bad Extension. KFF Ignorable filter hides known-good OS files. It shrinks the list dramatically. On any hit: Bottom viewer: Natural / Text / Hex / Filtered Right-click → Create Bookmark (name it after the finding, add a comment) Right-click → Export to hash elsewhere or feed to Vol / strings Registry hives: right-click → View in Registry Viewer (separate tool) for Run keys, UserAssist, ShellBags Pivot on the timestamp: sort the Explore list for that directory by Modified Report: File → Report → include Bookmarks only, plus hashes and file paths → HTML or PDF."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"8-autopsy","h":"8. Autopsy","p":"","t":""},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"8a-setup","h":"8A. Setup","p":"8. Autopsy","t":"New case: Case → New Case → name CASE_<host> → base directory on output drive → Next → examiner info → Finish Add Data Source wizard opens. Host: Generate new host name based on data source name → Next. Data source type: Disk Image or VM File — E01, raw/dd, VMDK, VHD (most common) Local Disk — a mounted or attached drive (FTK-mounted image works here) Logical Files — a folder of exported artifacts Pick the file → time zone = the target's time zone → Next. To display times in UTC: Tools → Options → View → time zone → GMT/UTC . Ingest modules. Fast set first, slow ones later. Run first (fast) Run later if time (slow) Recent Activity Keyword Search (full index) File Type Identification PhotoRec Carver Hash Lookup (+ NSRL if loaded) Plaso (full timeline) Extension Mismatch Detector Embedded File Extractor Interesting Files Identifier Encryption Detection Data Source Integrity YARA Analyzer (if present) Finish. Ingest runs in the background. Watch the progress bar bottom-right. Results appear as they finish, so start browsing immediately. Add a quick keyword list: Tools → Options → Keyword Search → New List → add terms (attacker IP, filename, base64 , eval( ) → run Keyword Search ingest against the data source. Memory images: if Add Data Source offers a memory/Volatility option, it runs embedded Vol2 plugins on Windows images only. Otherwise do memory in Vol3 (§4–§5) and bring dumped files back as Logical Files . Run ingest again later: right-click data source → Run Ingest Modules ."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"8b-hunt-navigation","h":"8B. Hunt Navigation","p":"8. Autopsy","t":"Tree layout (left). Work it top-down. Autopsy 4.19+ labels these Data Artifacts / Analysis Results ; older builds put them all under Results → Extracted Content . Node Use it for Data Sources Raw file system browse (same as FTK tree) File Views → File Types → By Extension / MIME All executable , .ps1 , .bat , .php , .sh at once File Views → Deleted Files Recently deleted tooling File Views → File Size Large archives (staging / exfil) Data Artifacts → Run Programs Prefetch-based execution Data Artifacts → Recent Documents · Shell Bags What a user opened / browsed Data Artifacts → Installed Programs · USB Device Attached New software, removable media Data Artifacts → Web History / Downloads How a payload arrived Data Artifacts → Operating System User Account Accounts, last logon Analysis Results → Extension Mismatch .jpg that's really a PE Analysis Results → Interesting Items · Hashset Hits Rule and hash matches Analysis Results → Keyword Hits Your keyword lists Tags Your bookmarked evidence (feeds the report) Timeline: Tools → Timeline . Set the zoom to the window around a known-bad time. Filter by event type (file modified, program run, web). Look for clusters around the suspicious timestamp. This is the fastest way to \"what happened right before and after.\" Workflow on any hit: Select the item → bottom pane: Hex / Text / File Metadata / Analysis Results Right-click → Add File Tag → Notable Item (comment why) Right-click → View File in Directory to see its neighbors Right-click → Extract File(s) to hash or feed to other tools Note the timestamp → jump to Timeline Report: Generate Report → HTML (or Excel). Choose Tagged results only for a clean findings export."},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"9-findings-log-keep-while-you-work","h":"9. Findings Log (keep while\nyou work)","p":"","t":"Keep it in Hunt Notes ( tools/hunt-notes.html ): paste the time as shown, pick the source zone, it stores UTC and builds the timeline, IOC list and coverage grid. Its Output tab prints this same format. Paper fallback, one line per observation: TIME (UTC) | HOST | SOURCE | Q | CONF | OBSERVATION | EVIDENCE / CMD | NEXT ---------------------+--------+-----------------+----+----------+------------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------- 2026-10-21 14:05:12Z | WIN-01 | live:Win32_Proc | Q1 | Possible | powershell -enc child of winword (4412) | Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | netscan 4412 2026-10-21 14:05:40Z | WIN-01 | vol3:netscan | Q2 | Probable | 4412 -> 203.0.113.7:443 ESTABLISHED | windows.netscan | dump 4412 2026-10-21 03:58:02Z | LNX-01 | access.log | Q4 | Possible | POST /uploads/x.php from 198.51.100.4 | access.log L8812 | find x.php Report-out per finding: FINDING: <one sentence> HOST: <name / IP> WHEN: <first seen UTC> → <last seen UTC> EVIDENCE: <artifact + tool + location> VALIDATION: <second, independent source that confirms it> ATT&CK: <tactic / technique ID> MITIGATION: <contain / remove / harden>"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"10-detection-kql-host-hunts","h":"10. Detection — KQL Host\nHunts","p":"","t":"Run in Kibana Discover, as saved searches, or as the basis for detection rules. KQL unless marked ES|QL. # Office / email spawning a shell (T1566 / T1059) process.parent.name:(\"winword.exe\" or \"excel.exe\" or \"powerpnt.exe\" or \"outlook.exe\") and process.name:(\"cmd.exe\" or \"powershell.exe\" or \"wscript.exe\" or \"cscript.exe\" or \"mshta.exe\") # Encoded / download-cradle PowerShell (T1059.001 / T1105) process.name:\"powershell.exe\" and process.command_line: (*-enc* or *-EncodedCommand* or *FromBase64String* or *IEX* or *Invoke-Expression* or *DownloadString* or *Net.WebClient* or *-nop* or *-w hidden*) # LOLBins with suspicious args (T1218) process.name:(\"rundll32.exe\" or \"regsvr32.exe\" or \"mshta.exe\" or \"certutil.exe\" or \"bitsadmin.exe\" or \"msiexec.exe\") and process.command_line:(*http* or *\\\\temp\\\\* or *-urlcache* or *javascript:* or *.dll,*) # LSASS access — credential dumping (T1003.001) Sysmon 10 / Defend event.code:\"10\" and winlog.event_data.TargetImage:*lsass.exe* and not winlog.event_data.SourceImage:(\"*\\\\MsMpEng.exe\" or \"*\\\\wininit.exe\") # New service / scheduled task persistence (T1543.003 / T1053.005) event.code:(\"7045\" or \"4697\" or \"4698\" or \"4702\") # Lateral movement via explicit creds (T1021 / T1078) then pivot one user.name -> many host.name event.code:\"4648\" # Failed-logon spray / bruteforce (T1110) aggregate by source.ip / user.name, threshold on count event.code:\"4625\" # Linux: suspicious execution from web/temp dirs (T1505.003 webshell / T1059.004) host.os.type:\"linux\" and process.executable:(\"/tmp/*\" or \"/dev/shm/*\" or \"/var/www/*\") and process.parent.name:(\"apache2\" or \"httpd\" or \"nginx\" or \"php-fpm\")"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"11-detection-kql-es-ql-network-hunts","h":"11. Detection — KQL /\nES|QL Network Hunts","p":"","t":"# Beaconing candidates — fixed-interval small conns to one external dst (T1071) ES|QL: FROM logs-zeek.* | WHERE network.direction == \"outbound\" | STATS conns = COUNT(*), bytes = SUM(network.bytes), uniq_dur = COUNT_DISTINCT(zeek.conn.duration) BY source.ip, destination.ip, destination.port | WHERE conns > 20 AND bytes < 500000 | SORT conns DESC # Rare external destinations (first-seen) destination.ip:* and not destination.ip:(<internal ranges>) and not destination.ip:(<baseline externals>) # DNS tunneling / DGA markers (T1071.004 / T1568) zeek.dns.query:* and (dns_qlen > 50 or dns_entropy > 4) zeek.dns.qtype_name:\"TXT\" dns.response_code:\"NXDOMAIN\" # Self-signed / odd TLS (T1573 / C2 over TLS) tls.server.x509.issuer.distinguished_name : tls.server.x509.subject.distinguished_name or tls.client.ja3:(<known-bad JA3>) or (tls.client.server_name:* and not destination.ip:<expected for that SNI>) # Long-lived external sessions zeek.conn.duration > 3600 and network.direction:\"outbound\""},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"12-detection-suricata","h":"12. Detection — Suricata","p":"","t":"Rule anatomy: action proto src_ip src_port -> dst_ip dst_port (msg:\"...\"; flow:...; content:\"...\"; \\ pcre:\"...\"; classtype:...; sid:1000001; rev:1; metadata:...;) Custom examples (defensive, terrain-specific): # Outbound cleartext to a known-bad / unexpected external on a monitored egress alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:\"CUSTOM Egress to flagged host\"; \\ flow:established,to_server; ip_dst <flagged-ip>; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9000001; rev:1;) # Self-signed TLS to external (metadata) alert tls $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:\"CUSTOM Self-signed cert outbound\"; \\ tls.cert_issuer; content:!\"CN=\"; classtype:protocol-command-decode; sid:9000002; rev:1;) # SMB lateral indicator (admin share from a workstation subnet) alert smb $WKSTN_NET any -> $SERVER_NET 445 (msg:\"CUSTOM Wkstn->Server admin share\"; \\ flow:to_server; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9000004; rev:1;) Baseline suppression (threshold.config) — every suppress cites its BL-ID: # Suppress a sig entirely from the range NAT/jump source (BL-003 [RANGE]) suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2000419, track by_src, ip <range-nat-ip> # Rate-limit a noisy-but-real sig to one alert per 5 min per host threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 9000003, type threshold, track by_src, count 1, seconds 300"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"13-detection-zeek","h":"13. Detection — Zeek","p":"","t":"Log What it gives Hunt use conn.log every flow: duration, bytes, state, history beaconing, long conns, top talkers, exfil ratios dns.log queries, responses, rcodes, types DGA, tunneling, NXDOMAIN spikes, TXT volume http.log method, host, URI, UA, status webshell URIs, rare UAs, suspicious downloads ssl.log + x509.log SNI, versions, cert subject/issuer, JA3/JA3S self-signed, rare JA3, SNI/IP mismatch files.log files seen, MIME, hashes malware delivery, carving notice.log / weird.log Zeek detections / protocol anomalies pre-built notices, tunneling, evasion Notice framework — turn a hunt into a standing detection: # long-conns.zeek — notice on outbound sessions over 1h @load base/frameworks/notice redef enum Notice::Type += { Long_Outbound_Conn }; event connection_state_remove(c: connection) { if ( c$duration > 1hr && Site::is_local_addr(c$id$orig_h) && ! Site::is_local_addr(c$id$resp_h) ) NOTICE([$note=Long_Outbound_Conn, $conn=c, $msg=fmt(\"Long outbound conn %s -> %s (%s)\", c$id$orig_h, c$id$resp_h, c$duration)]); } Baseline exclusion — suppress the ism in the notice policy, keep the log, cite the register ID: hook Notice::policy(n: Notice::Info) { if ( n$note == Long_Outbound_Conn && n$conn$id$resp_h == <backup-server> ) break; # BL-014 [BOTH] confirmed: nightly backup, not a finding }"},{"d":"Execution Reference","s":"execution-reference","a":"14-detection-elastic-defend","h":"14. Detection — Elastic\nDefend","p":"","t":"Mechanism What it does Use for Event filter Stops matching events being written/ingested at the agent High-volume known-good (range orchestration, traffic-generator noise). Cheapest, earliest. Rule exception Stops one rule alerting on a match, event still ingested Known-good that trips one rule but the raw event still has hunt value. Trusted application Excludes a binary/path/signer from protections A sanctioned tool the malware/behavior engine flags. # Event filter (BL-007 [RANGE] traffic generator) event.category : \"process\" and process.parent.name : \"<traffic-gen>.exe\" # Rule exception on \"Certutil URL download\" (BL-022 [CLIENT] confirmed admin host) host.name : \"ADM-TOOLS-01\" and user.name : \"svc-patch\""},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"","h":"FTK Runbook","p":"","t":"Linear disk investigation in FTK Imager + FTK, aligned to the five questions Run top to bottom. Each step says what to do, what to look for, and what to record. Tool menus shift between FTK 7 and 8; the order holds. Commands and path tables live in the Execution Reference (§3, §7). Record everything in Hunt Notes with the source FTK or FTK Imager . Where FTK fits. Memory first (Volatility), live triage second, disk third. Disk work in FTK proves and dates what memory and live triage found (Q1–Q3) and answers Q4–Q5. You arrive at step 1 with a lead list: names, paths, IPs, hashes, accounts. FTK searches for them; it does not replace them."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-0-pre-flight-5-min","h":"Step 0 — Pre-flight (5 min)","p":"","t":"FTK database service running; you can log in (FTK won't open a case without it). Credentials come from whoever built the box. Ask, don't guess. Version: Help → About . Note it. Target host's system time zone known (from live triage, §1.1 / §2.1). You need it in step 3. Output drive/path chosen. Nothing gets written to evidence media. Lead list open in Hunt Notes (IOC tab). These are your search terms in step 6."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-1-acquire-ftk-imager-10-20-min","h":"Step 1 — Acquire (FTK\nImager) (10–20 min)","p":"","t":"Skip if an image is provided. Go to step 2. Memory first if the box is live and not yet captured: File → Capture Memory → output path → HOST_mem.mem → Include pagefile (optional) → Capture. Hash it. Locked hives fast (if you need the registry before a full image): File → Obtain Protected Files → Password recovery and all registry files . Disk: File → Create Disk Image → Physical Drive (whole disk) or Logical Drive (one volume) → destination type E01 → evidence info → check Verify images after they are created → Start. Keep the generated .txt log (MD5/SHA-1). Record: image name, hashes, acquisition time (UTC). This is the integrity reference for every finding."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-2-verify-2-min","h":"Step 2 — Verify (2 min)","p":"","t":"Provided image: FTK Imager → File → Add Evidence Item → Image File → right-click → Verify Drive/Image . Hash must match the provided value or the acquisition log. Record: \"image verified, MD5/SHA-1 match\" with the time. If it doesn't match, stop and report. Analysis of an unverified image is not defensible."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-3-create-the-case-and-process-10-min-setup-processing-r","h":"Step\n3 — Create the case and process (10 min setup, processing runs in\nbackground)","p":"","t":"File → New Case → name CASE_<host> → case folder on the output drive. Processing profile Forensics (not Field Mode, which skips indexing). Customize: Turn on Leave off (time sinks) MD5 + SHA-1 OCR Flag Bad Extensions (file signature analysis) Explicit Image Detection Expand Compound Files Data Carving (add later in step 9 if needed) dtSearch Text Index Video thumbnails KFF, if a library is configured Cerberus, unless time allows Registry Reports Add evidence: Acquired Image of Drive → the E01/raw. Time zone = the target's system time zone. Wrong zone shifts every timestamp you report. OK → processing starts. Browse while it runs : steps 4–5 work on partial results. Index Search (step 6) is incomplete until indexing finishes. Record: case created, profile, zone set, processing start time."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-4-fast-triage-filters-overview-tab-10-min","h":"Step 4 — Fast\ntriage filters (Overview tab) (10 min)","p":"","t":"Shrinks the disk to the files that matter. Work in this order: # Click Look for Feeds a Overview → KFF Alert Known-bad hash hits. Fastest win if a library is loaded Q1 b Overview → Bad Extension Renamed executables ( .jpg that is a PE) Q1 c Overview → File Category → Executable Sort Created/Modified desc. Anything in Users\\ , Temp , ProgramData , Public , /tmp , /var/www Q1, Q5 d Overview → Deleted Tools removed after use Q1, Q5 e Overview → Encrypted / high entropy Packed payloads, staged archives Q1 Turn on the KFF Ignorable filter to hide known-good OS files. Add columns (right-click header): Created, Modified, Accessed, Path, MD5, Bad Extension. Record: each hit as an entry (time = the file's Created or Modified time in the zone you set, Q1). Bookmark it (step 10 method)."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-5-q1-what-is-running-that-should-not-be-prove-it-on-dis","h":"Step\n5 — Q1: What is running that should not be? Prove it on disk (15\nmin)","p":"","t":"For each process lead from memory/live triage: Explore tab → navigate to its path. Does the file exist? Size, MD5, Created/Modified. Bottom viewer → Hex : MZ = PE, 7F 45 4C 46 = ELF. Header vs extension. Right-click → Export → hash it SHA-256 on the analysis box ( Get-FileHash / sha256sum ). Hash-match: filter on that MD5 to find copies elsewhere on the image (staging dirs, other profiles). Gate G1: unsigned, writable path, or bad parent + file present on disk = acquire and hash. Record: file path, hashes, times. Memory process + disk file = two sources , Confirmed for Q1."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-6-index-search-your-lead-list-10-min-once-indexing-is-d","h":"Step\n6 — Index Search your lead list (10 min, once indexing is done)","p":"","t":"Index Search tab → one term per line from Hunt Notes IOCs: IPs, domains, filenames, service/task names, account names, webshell strings ( eval( , base64_decode ). Use Live Search for regex or hex signatures (slower, no index needed). On each hit: open the containing file, note path + timestamps, bookmark. Record: hit location, Q matching what it shows."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-7-q3-how-does-it-survive-a-reboot-15-min","h":"Step 7 — Q3: How\ndoes it survive a reboot? (15 min)","p":"","t":"Explore to the persistence locations (Execution Reference §7B path tables): Windows Linux Windows\\System32\\config\\SOFTWARE , SYSTEM → right-click → View in Registry Viewer → Run/RunOnce, Services (ImagePath, ServiceDll), Winlogon /etc/cron* , /var/spool/cron/crontabs/ Users\\<u>\\NTUSER.DAT → Run, RunOnce /etc/systemd/system/ , /lib/systemd/system/ (enabled = symlink in *.wants/ ) Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\ (task XML: <Exec><Command> ) /root/ and /home/*/.bashrc , .profile , .ssh/authorized_keys ProgramData\\...\\StartUp\\ , Users\\<u>\\AppData\\Roaming\\...\\Startup\\ /etc/ld.so.preload , /etc/rc.local Windows\\System32\\wbem\\Repository\\ (WMI, needs parser) /var/www/ (webshell is persistence too) Gate G3: a persistence entry that points at the Q1 file = foothold confirmed . Record: entry, its target, the key/file last-write time (Q3)."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-8-q4-and-q5-who-how-in-and-when-it-started-20-min","h":"Step 8\n— Q4 and Q5: Who, how in, and when it started (20 min)","p":"","t":"Q4 (who / how): export the logs and read them with the Execution Reference queries. Windows: Windows\\System32\\winevt\\Logs\\ → export Security.evtx , System.evtx , Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell%4Operational.evtx , Sysmon. On the analysis box: Get-WinEvent -Path .\\Security.evtx -FilterXPath '*[System[(EventID=4624 or EventID=4648 or EventID=4672)]]' . Users\\ → which profile owns the Q1 file? SAM in Registry Viewer → accounts, last logon. Linux: /var/log/auth.log* , /var/log/apache2/access.log* , /etc/passwd (UID 0), /home/*/.bash_history . Q5 (when): start-time anchors, in priority order: Windows\\Prefetch\\<NAME>-<HASH>.pf : created ≈ first run. Export; times show in File List. Windows\\appcompat\\Programs\\Amcache.hve → Registry Viewer → Root\\InventoryApplicationFile : entry for the file, SHA-1, key last-write. NTUSER.DAT → ...\\Explorer\\UserAssist\\{GUID}\\Count (names are ROT13); SYSTEM → ...\\Services\\bam\\State\\UserSettings\\<SID> . The file's own $MFT times last. They are the first thing altered. Check timestomping in Autopsy (File Metadata tab shows $SI vs $FN ) or export $MFT . Gate G4: account + auth method found = scope extends to other hosts. Gate G5: first-seen earlier than the alert = intrusion start. Record: Q4 and Q5 entries, each citing the artifact."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-9-optional-depth-only-if-time-remains","h":"Step 9 — Optional\ndepth (only if time remains)","p":"","t":"Evidence → Additional Analysis → Data Carving (executables, archives, scripts only). Carved files have no path and no reliable timestamps ; corroborate before using. Alternate Data Streams appear in the file list; check them on suspicious files. $Recycle.Bin\\<SID>\\ → $I (original path + delete time) / $R (content)."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"step-10-bookmark-and-report-10-min","h":"Step 10 — Bookmark and\nreport (10 min)","p":"","t":"On every hit as you go: right-click → Create Bookmark → name it after the finding ( T-04 x.exe in Public ) → comment = why it matters + Q. Report: File → Report → Bookmarks only , include hashes and paths → HTML or PDF. Write each finding with the report-out format (Execution Reference §9): finding, host, first/last seen UTC, evidence, second independent source , ATT&CK, mitigation."},{"d":"FTK Runbook","s":"ftk-runbook","a":"pitfalls","h":"Pitfalls","p":"","t":"Analyzing before verifying the image hash. Wrong time zone on the evidence item. Every reported time is then off by the offset. Waiting for processing to finish before looking at anything. Browse Overview while it runs. Trusting carved-file timestamps or paths (there are none). Expecting FTK to decode Prefetch/Amcache/Shimcache into a timeline. It locates and exports; Registry Viewer, Volatility, or Autopsy interpret. Doing memory analysis in FTK. Capture here, analyze in Volatility, add dumped files back as Individual File evidence."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"","h":"Autopsy Runbook","p":"","t":"Linear disk investigation in Autopsy 4.x, aligned to the five questions Run top to bottom. Each step says what to do, what to look for, and what to record. Autopsy 4.19+ labels the tree Data Artifacts / Analysis Results ; older builds put everything under Results → Extracted Content . Path tables are in the Execution Reference §7B. Record in Hunt Notes with source Autopsy . Where Autopsy fits. Same role as FTK: disk proves and dates what memory and live triage found (Q1–Q3) and answers Q4–Q5. Autopsy's advantages are that it decodes execution artifacts for you (Run Programs, Recent Activity), has a built-in Timeline , and shows $SI vs $FN times per file. Use it for Q5 even if FTK did the rest."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-0-pre-flight-5-min","h":"Step 0 — Pre-flight (5 min)","p":"","t":"Version: Help → About . Note it. Target host's system time zone known (live triage §1.1 / §2.1). Image hash verified (FTK Imager Verify Drive/Image , or the provided hash). Output drive/path chosen. Lead list from Hunt Notes ready: names, paths, IPs, hashes, accounts."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-1-create-the-case-3-min","h":"Step 1 — Create the case (3\nmin)","p":"","t":"Case → New Case → name CASE_<host> → base directory on the output drive → Next → examiner info → Finish. Add Data Source opens. Host: Generate new host name based on data source name → Next. Type: Disk Image or VM File (E01, raw/dd, VMDK, VHD). Use Local Disk for a mounted image and Logical Files for a folder of exported artifacts. Select the image → time zone = the target's → Next."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-2-ingest-fast-set-first-5-min-setup-runs-in-background","h":"Step 2\n— Ingest: fast set first (5 min setup, runs in background)","p":"","t":"Run now Add later if time (right-click data source → Run Ingest Modules) Recent Activity Keyword Search (full index) File Type Identification PhotoRec Carver Hash Lookup (+ NSRL if loaded) Plaso (full super-timeline, slow) Extension Mismatch Detector Embedded File Extractor Interesting Files Identifier Encryption Detection Data Source Integrity YARA Analyzer (if present) Load your lead list as keywords before running Keyword Search: Tools → Options → Keyword Search → New List → add IPs, filenames, base64_decode , eval( → run Keyword Search ingest on the data source. Display in UTC: Tools → Options → View → time zone GMT/UTC . Then times you read go into Hunt Notes with zone UTC , no conversion. Results populate as modules finish. Start step 3 immediately."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-3-fast-triage-10-min","h":"Step 3 — Fast triage (10 min)","p":"","t":"# Tree node Look for Feeds a Analysis Results → Hashset Hits Known-bad hash matches Q1 b Analysis Results → Extension Mismatch .jpg that is a PE Q1 c Analysis Results → Interesting Items Rule matches (from Interesting Files Identifier) Q1 d File Views → File Types → By Extension → Executable, Scripts ( .ps1 .bat .vbs .php .sh ) Sort Modified desc. User, Temp, ProgramData, Public, /tmp , /var/www Q1, Q5 e File Views → Deleted Files Tools removed after use Q1, Q5 f File Views → File Size Large archives (staging / exfil) Q1 On every hit: right-click → Add File Tag → Notable Item → comment = why + Q. Record in Hunt Notes."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-4-q1-what-is-running-that-should-not-be-prove-it-on-dis","h":"Step\n4 — Q1: What is running that should not be? Prove it on disk (15\nmin)","p":"","t":"For each process lead from memory/live triage: Data Sources tree → navigate to the path. File present? Bottom pane → File Metadata : size, MD5/SHA-256, times. Hex tab: MZ = PE, 7F 45 4C 46 = ELF. Right-click → View File in Directory : neighbors dropped at the same time. Right-click → Extract File(s) → hash on the analysis box. Gate G1 → acquire and hash. Memory process + disk file = two sources , Confirmed for Q1."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-5-q3-how-does-it-survive-a-reboot-15-min","h":"Step 5 — Q3: How\ndoes it survive a reboot? (15 min)","p":"","t":"Data Artifacts → Run Programs / Installed Programs / Operating System Information. New software, odd service binaries. Data Sources → Windows\\System32\\config\\SOFTWARE / SYSTEM / Users\\<u>\\NTUSER.DAT → select → Application tab shows the registry tree. Check Run/RunOnce, Services ( ImagePath , Parameters\\ServiceDll ), Winlogon. Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\ → task XML → Text tab → <Command> . Linux: /etc/cron* , /var/spool/cron/crontabs/ , /etc/systemd/system/*.wants/ , /home/*/.ssh/authorized_keys , /etc/ld.so.preload , /var/www/ . Gate G3: persistence pointing at the Q1 file = foothold confirmed . Record entry, target, last-write time (Q3)."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-6-q4-who-did-it-and-how-did-they-get-in-15-min","h":"Step 6 —\nQ4: Who did it, and how did they get in? (15 min)","p":"","t":"Data Artifacts → Operating System User Account : accounts, created, last logon. Data Artifacts → Web History / Web Downloads : how a payload arrived (browser download, URL). Data Artifacts → Recent Documents · Shell Bags · USB Device Attached : user activity, removable media. Event logs: Data Sources → Windows\\System32\\winevt\\Logs\\ → Extract Security/System/PowerShell/Sysmon .evtx → query on the analysis box (Execution Reference §1.7 queries with -Path ). Linux: extract /var/log/auth.log* , /var/log/apache2/access.log* , .bash_history → Execution Reference §2.5 / §2.7 greps. Gate G4: account + auth method = scope extends to other hosts. Record (Q4)."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-7-q5-when-did-it-start-20-min","h":"Step 7 — Q5: When did it\nstart? (20 min)","p":"","t":"This is where Autopsy beats FTK. Data Artifacts → Run Programs : Prefetch-derived executions with run times and counts. Find the Q1 binary. Amcache / UserAssist / BAM : Recent Activity parses these into Data Artifacts (Run Programs / Recent Activity nodes, depending on version). If not shown, extract Amcache.hve / NTUSER.DAT / SYSTEM and use the native reads in Execution Reference §1.8. Timestomp check: select the file → File Metadata tab. It shows $STANDARD_INFORMATION and $FILE_NAME times. SI earlier than FN, or SI with zero sub-seconds = timestomped; trust FN and the execution artifacts. Timeline: Tools → Timeline → zoom to the window around the earliest time from 1–3 → filter event types (file created/modified, program run, web). Read what happened just before : download, logon, web POST, archive extraction. Gate G5: first-seen earlier than the alert = intrusion start. Two artifacts agreeing on first run = confirmed start. Record each anchor (Q5), citing the artifact."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-8-optional-depth-only-if-time-remains","h":"Step 8 — Optional\ndepth (only if time remains)","p":"","t":"Run PhotoRec Carver / Plaso ingest. Carved files have no path and no reliable time; corroborate. $Recycle.Bin\\<SID>\\ → $I (original path + delete time) / $R (content). Analysis Results → Encryption Detected (packed / encrypted payloads)."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"step-9-report-10-min","h":"Step 9 — Report (10 min)","p":"","t":"Tags node → review every Notable Item. Generate Report → HTML (or Excel) → Tagged results only . Write each finding with the Execution Reference §9 report-out: finding, host, first/last seen UTC, evidence, second independent source , ATT&CK, mitigation."},{"d":"Autopsy Runbook","s":"autopsy-runbook","a":"pitfalls","h":"Pitfalls","p":"","t":"Wrong time zone at Add Data Source. Every reported time is off by the offset. Waiting for ingest to finish. Browse triage nodes as they fill. Running every ingest module up front and losing an hour to carving/Plaso. Trusting $SI times without the File Metadata $FN check. Memory images: Autopsy's Volatility option is Vol2 and Windows-only. Do memory in Vol3 and add dumped files back as Logical Files ."},{"d":"Sysinternals Reference","s":"sysinternals","a":"","h":"Sysinternals Reference","p":"","t":"What to run, what in the output matters, and the native fallback, ordered by the five questions Use Sysinternals when it's on the bench. It sees more than the built-ins (signatures, hashes, every autostart location at once). Every tool has a native fallback in the Execution Reference, so nothing here is a single point of failure. Footprint. First run of each tool writes an EULA-accepted key under HKCU\\Software\\Sysinternals . Pass -accepteula and note it in Hunt Notes (\"ran autorunsc, EULA key written\"). Run from your tools media, write output to your output path, never to the evidence volume. Everything below is read-only. Never run sdelete , psexec against other hosts, or pskill on evidence. Run order on a live Windows host (after memory capture): 1 autorunsc (Q3) 2 sigcheck (Q1) 3 tcpvcon (Q2) 4 pslist -t / Process Explorer (Q1) 5 listdlls -u / handle on suspects (Q1) 6 logonsessions / psloggedon (Q4) 7 streams / strings on files (Q1/Q5)"},{"d":"Sysinternals Reference","s":"sysinternals","a":"q1-what-is-running-that-should-not-be","h":"Q1 — What is running\nthat should not be?","p":"","t":"Process Explorer ( procexp64.exe , GUI) Options → Verify Image Signatures . Add columns ( View → Select Columns ): Image Path, Command Line, Verified Signer, Company Name, Start Time . Tree view = parentage. Look for Office/browser → cmd / powershell , svchost not under services.exe , lsass not under wininit . Colors: purple = packed/compressed image, pink = service, light blue = same user as you. Suspect PID → lower pane ( Ctrl+L ): DLL view (unsigned or user-path DLLs) and Handle view (files, mutexes, keys). Properties → TCP/IP tab (its connections), Strings tab (Image vs Memory; memory strings reveal unpacked content). Fallback: Execution Reference §1.2. PsList (CLI) pslist -accepteula -t # tree with parent/child pslist -accepteula -x <PID> # threads + memory detail for one PID Sigcheck : unsigned executables in user-writable paths, with hashes: sigcheck64 -accepteula -nobanner -e -u -s -h -c C:\\Users C:\\ProgramData C:\\Windows\\Temp > sigcheck.csv -e executables only · -u unsigned only · -s recurse · -h hashes · -c CSV. Look at Path + Publisher + SHA256. Anything unsigned in AppData , Public , ProgramData , Temp is a Q1 lead. Fallback: §1.2 Get-AuthenticodeSignature . ListDLLs : unsigned DLLs loaded anywhere (DLL hijack / injection): listdlls64 -accepteula -u # unsigned DLLs, all processes listdlls64 -accepteula <PID|name> # everything one process loaded Fallback: Vol3 windows.dlllist --pid / windows.ldrmodules . Handle : what a suspect has open: handle64 -accepteula -nobanner -p <PID> # all handles for the PID handle64 -accepteula -nobanner -a <name-fragment> # who holds a file/mutex/key matching a string Fallback: Vol3 windows.handles --pid ."},{"d":"Sysinternals Reference","s":"sysinternals","a":"q2-what-is-talking-that-should-not-be","h":"Q2 — What is talking\nthat should not be?","p":"","t":"TCPView (GUI) / tcpvcon (CLI): connection → process, with the owning image path: tcpvcon64 -accepteula -a -c -n > tcpvcon.csv # all endpoints, CSV, no name resolution Look for: established sessions owned by processes with no network role ( notepad , rundll32 , Office), listening ports above 1024 owned by user-path binaries, the same remote IP from two hosts. Fallback: §1.3 Get-NetTCPConnection / netstat -anob ; memory: windows.netscan ."},{"d":"Sysinternals Reference","s":"sysinternals","a":"q3-how-does-it-survive-a-reboot","h":"Q3 — How does it survive a\nreboot?","p":"","t":"Autoruns (GUI) / autorunsc (CLI): every autostart location in one pass. This is the highest-value Sysinternals tool for a hunt. autorunsc64 -accepteula -nobanner -a * -c -h -s -m -t > autoruns.csv -a * all categories · -c CSV · -h hashes · -s verify signatures · -m hide Microsoft-signed · -t timestamps in UTC (straight into Hunt Notes). Read the CSV for: Signer = (Not verified) , Image Path in user-writable dirs, Launch String with powershell -enc , rundll32 <odd>.dll , mshta , wscript , cmd /c + a script in ProgramData. Categories that matter: Logon (Run keys), Services, Scheduled Tasks, WMI, Winlogon, Image Hijacks (IFEO), AppInit, Known DLLs, Boot Execute. GUI: Options → Hide Microsoft Entries + Verify Code Signatures . Pink = no publisher / unsigned, yellow = entry exists but file is missing (deleted tooling: still a Q3/Q5 lead). Entry → Jump to Entry opens the key or task. Fallback: §1.4 (Run keys, services, ServiceDll, tasks, WMI, Winlogon, IFEO, BITS). PsService : one service in detail: psservice64 -accepteula config <ServiceName> AccessChk : weak permissions that let a low-priv user plant persistence or escalate: accesschk64 -accepteula -nobanner -uwcqv \"Authenticated Users\" * # services anyone can reconfigure accesschk64 -accepteula -nobanner -uwsq Users \"C:\\Program Files\" # writable program dirs A writable service binary path or service config is a Q3/Q4 finding (and a hardening recommendation)."},{"d":"Sysinternals Reference","s":"sysinternals","a":"q4-who-did-it-and-how-did-they-get-in","h":"Q4 — Who did it, and how\ndid they get in?","p":"","t":"logonsessions64 -accepteula -nobanner -p # every logon session + its processes (logon type, auth package) psloggedon64 -accepteula # local and resource-share logons psinfo64 -accepteula -s -h # OS build, install date, software, hotfixes logonsessions -p ties a process to the session that started it: a network (type 3) or RemoteInteractive (type 10) session owning the Q1 process means the operator reached in. Fallback: §1.5 + §1.7 (4624/4648/4672)."},{"d":"Sysinternals Reference","s":"sysinternals","a":"q5-when-did-it-start-hidden-content","h":"Q5 — When did it start? /\nhidden content","p":"","t":"Streams : Alternate Data Streams (hidden payloads, Zone.Identifier download origin): streams64 -accepteula -nobanner -s C:\\Users\\Public streams64 -accepteula -nobanner <file> Zone.Identifier with ZoneId=3 + HostUrl= tells you where a file was downloaded from. Read it natively: Get-Content <file> -Stream Zone.Identifier . Strings : quick static look at a suspect binary (export it first; don't run it): strings64 -accepteula -nobanner -n 8 <file> | findstr /i \"http :// .onion cmd powershell \\\\pipe\\\\ HKEY\" Sigcheck for file metadata + hashes: sigcheck64 -accepteula -nobanner -a -h <file> ( -a adds original/internal name and entropy; high entropy = packed). Process Monitor ( procmon64.exe ): live behavior, only for a short, deliberate capture: Filter first ( Ctrl+L ): Process Name is <suspect> → Include. Or Operation is Process Create , RegSetValue , WriteFile . Tools → Process Tree (lineage + start/end times for the capture), Tools → File Summary / Registry Summary . Footprint: loads a driver and generates heavy I/O. Capture a minute, save .PML to the output path, stop. Note start/stop UTC in Hunt Notes. Sysmon : installing it changes the host. Do it only if authorized and noted. Reading an existing Sysmon log is in §1.7."},{"d":"Sysinternals Reference","s":"sysinternals","a":"output-hunt-notes","h":"Output → Hunt Notes","p":"","t":"Tool output Hunt Notes entry autorunsc row (unsigned, odd path) Q3, source autorunsc , time = its UTC timestamp, IOC = image path + SHA-256 sigcheck row Q1, source sigcheck , IOC = path + hash tcpvcon row Q2, source tcpvcon , IOC = remote IP:port, evidence = owning PID/image logonsessions Q4, source logonsessions , IOC = account, evidence = logon type + session ID Zone.Identifier Q4/Q5, source streams , IOC = HostUrl A Sysinternals result plus a memory (Volatility) or disk (FTK/Autopsy) result for the same item = two independent sources ."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"","h":"Analyst Methods Handbook","p":"","t":"Threat Hunt / DFIR Methodology Reference"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"collection-plan-methodology","h":"Collection Plan Methodology","p":"","t":"Purpose. Walk onto a network, look at how it is built and (when you have it) who is coming after it, and decide what to collect, where to collect it, how, and why — before you drown in data you can't use or miss the data you needed. This is the front of the pipeline: [ COLLECT ] -> [ BASELINE ] -> [ VISUALIZE ] -> [ DETECT ] collection plan what's normal Kibana dashes Suricata/Zeek/Defend/KQL Everything downstream inherits the decisions you make here. A dashboard can only show what you collected; a baseline can only normalize what you collected; a detection can only fire on what you collected. Collection is the first and most expensive mistake to get wrong."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"1-the-organizing-idea-collect-at-terrain-x-threat","h":"1. The\norganizing idea: collect at Terrain x Threat","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"The instinct on a new network is to \"collect everything.\" You can't, and you shouldn't — you have finite sensors, finite ingest, finite analysts, and some collection is actively harmful (crashing an OT device, tipping off an actor you're watching). So you need a principle that tells you where the value is . The value is at the intersection of terrain and threat: Terrain = what exists and what matters. The network build, the segments, the choke points, the crown jewels, the data flows. Terrain tells you where things can happen and where it would hurt. Threat = what the adversary does. Their TTPs, expressed as ATT&CK techniques, each of which leaves evidence in a specific place and data source. Threat tells you what to look for and what records it leaves. THREAT (what the adversary does) techniques -> required telemetry | v TERRAIN -------> [ CROSSWALK ] -------> COLLECTION REQUIREMENTS where things where a technique what / where / how / happen & hurt would land on YOUR why / priority / owner terrain, and what data source sees it Where a technique the threat uses would land on terrain you care about, and a data source exists that would see it — that is a collection requirement. Rank those requirements, place sensors and request logs to satisfy the top ones within your constraints, then prove your coverage back against both axes. Two framings feed the crosswalk and you use both: Threat-informed — start from the actor. \"APTxx uses T1021.002 (SMB/admin shares) for lateral movement\" -> you need SMB session + auth telemetry on the segments where lateral movement would occur. Precise, but only as good as your intel. Coverage-informed — start from the platform. \"This is a Windows AD environment\" -> there is a known default set of techniques and a known default telemetry set that covers most of them (process creation, auth, PowerShell, persistence, network). Broad, works with zero intel, and catches the actor your intel didn't name. Rule of thumb: coverage-informed sets your floor (you always collect the default visibility set for the platform); threat-informed raises specific areas above the floor (you collect extra where a named actor concentrates). If you have no intel, you still have a complete plan from the coverage side alone."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"2-inputs-what-you-gather-before-you-plan","h":"2. Inputs — what you\ngather before you plan","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"You can't crosswalk terrain against threat until you've modeled both. Collect these first: Network build / terrain Network diagram(s) — L2/L3, VLANs/segments, routing, egress points, DMZ, management network, OT/ICS boundary if any. Asset inventory — hosts, roles (DC, file server, web, DB, workstation, jump host), OS/version, owners, criticality. Data-flow / service map — who talks to whom, on what ports, in which direction; where the crown-jewel data lives and moves. Choke points — where traffic concentrates (egress, core/distribution, inter-segment routers, VPN concentrators). Existing visibility — what's already logging, what's already forwarding, where sensors already sit. Don't re-collect what you already have. Threat intel (when available) Named actor(s) and their TTPs -> translate to ATT&CK technique IDs. Known IOCs (hashes, domains, IPs, JA3/JA4) — these feed detection (intel framework / indicator matching), not collection placement, but note them. Targeted sectors/data — tells you which crown jewels are in play. If no intel : use the platform default technique set (coverage-informed). Don't stall waiting for intel. Engagement / constraints Objective — hunt known compromise? establish monitoring? validate after an incident? This changes priority weighting. ROE / authorities — what you are allowed to touch, deploy, decrypt, and collect. Hard boundary. Environment — range vs real client (drives your assumption markers, Section 7). Resources — sensor count (often one ), ingest budget, analyst hours, change-control friction."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"3-the-method-step-by-step","h":"3. The method, step by step","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"1. MAP TERRAIN enumerate assets, segments, choke points, crown jewels, flows -> terrain model 2. CHARACTERIZE intel -> ATT&CK techniques -> required data sources THREAT (no intel? platform default technique set) 3. CROSSWALK overlay each technique on terrain: where would it manifest here, what data source would see it? -> candidate collection requirements 4. PRIORITIZE score each = likelihood x detection-value x feasibility -> ranked requirement list 5. PLACE / REQUEST network: choose sensor location(s) (Section 5) host: request logs / deploy agents (Section 6) 6. DOCUMENT write each as a collection-requirement record (Section 8) -> the collection plan 7. VALIDATE map planned collection back onto ATT&CK + terrain; find and log the gaps (Section 10) Step 2 detail — technique to data source. Each ATT&CK technique has associated data sources / data components (ATT&CK publishes these). That mapping is the bridge from \"what the actor does\" to \"what I must collect.\" Examples: Technique (example) Primary data source(s) Where it lands T1059.001 PowerShell Script block logging (4104), process creation (Sysmon 1 / 4688), module logging Endpoint T1021.002 SMB/Admin shares Logon (4624 type 3), network share (5140/5145), SMB flows Endpoint + network T1071 App-layer C2 Network connection, DNS, TLS metadata, proxy/flow Network sensor T1053.005 Scheduled task Task events (4698/4702), Sysmon 1, registry Endpoint T1003 Cred dumping (LSASS) Process access (Sysmon 10), EDR behavioral, memory Endpoint (EDR) T1048 Exfil over alt proto Flow records, bytes-out, DNS, protocol metadata Network sensor The point of the table is the pattern : some techniques are only visible on the host, some only on the wire, many on both — and that tells you whether a requirement is a sensor-placement decision or a log-request/agent decision. Step 4 detail — the priority score. Rank every candidate requirement by three factors (1-5 each; multiply, or weight if you prefer): Threat likelihood — how probable is this technique on this terrain, given intel + platform? (No intel -> rate by prevalence on the platform.) Detection value — if you collect this, how much does it actually buy you? High for telemetry that covers many techniques (process creation, auth, DNS); low for narrow one-off sources. Feasibility — can you realistically collect it? Accounts for sensor access, ingest cost, change control, host fragility, ROE. A perfect requirement you can't satisfy scores zero in practice. priority = likelihood x detection_value x feasibility This forces the hard trade honestly: a high-value source you cannot feasibly collect drops below a medium-value source you can. You collect the plan you can actually execute, not the plan you wish you had."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"4-host-network-are-one-plan-not-two","h":"4. Host + network are one\nplan, not two","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"Keep them in one document because the same technique often shows on both, and the two collection surfaces cover each other's blind spots: The network sensor sees breadth cheaply (every host that crosses the sensor, no agent needed) but goes blind on encryption, on same-segment host-local activity, and on anything that never crosses the sensor. Host collection sees depth (process lineage, script content, in-memory behavior, local file writes) but only on hosts you've instrumented, and it's expensive per host. So the plan reads: network sensor for breadth and the wire; host collection to add depth where the terrain is valuable or the network sensor is blind. The two sections below are halves of the same decision."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"5-network-collection-sensor-placement","h":"5. Network collection —\nsensor placement","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":""},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"the-one-sensor-problem","h":"The one-sensor problem","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 5. Network collection —\nsensor placement","t":"Assume you get one sensor. Where it sits decides what the entire network side of your operation can see, so this is the highest-leverage single decision in the plan. The question is which axis of traffic you buy visibility into: NORTH (Internet / external) ^ | <-- egress / boundary choke: | sees C2 beacon-out, ingress, exfil, [ PERIMETER / EGRESS ] inbound exploit | ====================== <-- core / distribution choke: | sees east-west, lateral movement, [ CORE / DISTRIBUTION ] internal recon, crown-jewel access | [ ACCESS / HOSTS ] v SOUTH (internal) North-south (egress/boundary) catches the adversary's conversations with the outside world — C2 check-ins, data exfiltration, inbound exploitation, downloads. If the engagement is an external threat actor, C2, or exfil, this is usually the default. It is also the cheapest high-value spot because nearly all external-facing badness funnels through it. East-west (core/distribution) catches movement inside the perimeter — lateral movement, internal recon, credential reuse, access to crown jewels. If the engagement is an insider, an already-established foothold, or protecting specific internal assets, this axis wins."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"picking-the-one-spot-decision-logic","h":"Picking the one spot —\ndecision logic","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 5. Network collection —\nsensor placement","t":"Start. | Q1: Is the engagement external threat / C2 / exfil / unknown? | YES -> default to the EGRESS / boundary choke point. | NO -> continue. | Q2: Is the engagement internal (insider, established foothold, | protect specific crown jewels)? | YES -> default to the CORE / distribution choke that | carries the crown-jewel flows. | NO -> continue. | Q3: Is there ONE choke point that carries both the egress AND | the bulk of east-west? (small/flat networks often have this) | YES -> put it there; you get both axes at once. | NO -> fall back to egress (broadest default coverage). | Then adjust for feasibility (below) before committing."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"scoring-candidate-locations","h":"Scoring candidate locations","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 5. Network collection —\nsensor placement","t":"Rate each physical candidate and let feasibility veto: Coverage breadth — how many hosts/flows of interest cross this point. Choke centrality — does traffic concentrate here, or will you miss flows that route around it? Crown-jewel visibility — does the sensor see the traffic to/from what matters most? Decryption availability — can you see inside TLS here (proxy break, keys), or only metadata? Placement near a decryption point is worth a lot. Feasibility — SPAN capacity on the switch, physical TAP access, throughput/oversubscription, asymmetric routing (do you see both directions?), and whether you're allowed to tap at all."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"the-secondary-sensor","h":"The secondary sensor","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 5. Network collection —\nsensor placement","t":"If scope allows a second sensor, do not duplicate the first — cover the first one's blind spot. The whole value of #2 is complementary coverage: If #1 is at egress (north-south), put #2 at the core/distribution for east-west, or on the segment holding the crown jewels. If #1 is at the core, put #2 at egress to pick up C2/exfil. Special case: if there's an OT/ICS boundary or a high-value enclave, #2 on that boundary is often worth more than generic east-west — it's terrain you can't afford to be blind to and usually can't instrument at the host."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"practical-wire-notes-carry-these-on-site","h":"Practical wire notes\n(carry these on-site)","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 5. Network collection —\nsensor placement","t":"TAP vs SPAN — TAP is passive, full-fidelity, no drops, needs physical access + the right media. SPAN is config-only but drops under load, can't exceed the port's bandwidth, and may miss errors. Prefer TAP for a monitored choke; SPAN when you can't physically tap. Aggregation — a packet broker / aggregation TAP combines multiple links and feeds one sensor; useful when the \"one choke\" is really several links. Asymmetric routing — confirm you see both directions of a flow, or stateful tools (Suricata/Zeek) misparse sessions. NAT — a sensor outside NAT sees the translated (public) address and loses the internal host; inside NAT you keep host attribution. Prefer inside, or collect both and correlate. TLS — without decryption you still get rich metadata: JA3/JA4 (client/server fingerprints), SNI, certificate subject/issuer/validity, cert chains. Plan to collect these even when you can't see payload. Time — make sure the sensor's clock is NTP-synced to the same source as the hosts, or correlation across host/network falls apart."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"6-host-collection-what-to-request-vs-deploy","h":"6. Host collection\n— what to request vs deploy","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"Two categories, and the distinction drives the action: Already generated -> request / forward. The host is already writing these; you just need them shipped (Winlogbeat / Elastic Agent / syslog). Low risk, no new software behavior. Not generated yet -> deploy. You have to add something (Sysmon config, an EDR agent, auditd rules) to produce the telemetry. Higher risk, change control, host impact."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"windows-request-first","h":"Windows — request first","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 6. Host collection\n— what to request vs deploy","t":"EVTX, the core set (request + forward to Kibana via Winlogbeat/Elastic Agent): Security (4624/4625 logon, 4648 explicit-cred lateral, 4672 priv, 4688 process w/ cmdline if enabled, 4697 service install, 4698/4702 scheduled task, 4720/4728/4732 account+group changes, 5140/5145 share access, 4768/4769/4771 Kerberos). System (7045 service install, 7035/7036 service control). Application (app crashes, install MSI events). Sysmon (if present) — 1 process w/ hashes+cmdline+parent, 3 network, 7 image load, 8 remote thread, 10 process access (LSASS), 11 file create, 13 registry, 22 DNS. Sysmon is the single highest-value Windows host source; if it isn't deployed, that's a deploy decision below. PowerShell — 4104 script block (deobfuscated script content), 4103 module logging, transcription. Windows Defender / AV operational log, WMI-Activity, TaskScheduler operational, TerminalServices (RDP), Windows Firewall. Dead-box / triage artifacts (request as a collection or pull with a triage tool — Velociraptor/KAPE if ROE allows): $MFT, USN journal, Amcache/Shimcache, Prefetch, registry hives (SYSTEM/SOFTWARE/SAM/NTUSER), SRUM, Scheduled Tasks XML, Autoruns, browser history, memory image for the live-response cases. Server app logs — IIS, Apache/Nginx on Windows, DB logs, Exchange, anything web-facing (webshell hunting)."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"linux-request-first","h":"Linux — request first","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 6. Host collection\n— what to request vs deploy","t":"auth ( /var/log/auth.log / secure ), journald , and auditd (execve, file, network syscalls — auditd is the Linux equivalent of Sysmon in value; if not configured, it's a deploy decision). Shell history ( .bash_history , with timestamps if HISTTIMEFORMAT set), cron , /etc/ change evidence, package-manager logs. Web/app logs — Apache/Nginx access+error (webshell + exploitation), app logs, DB logs. Running state for live response — processes, listening sockets, loaded modules, /proc , open files."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"ingest-note","h":"Ingest note","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 6. Host collection\n— what to request vs deploy","t":"EVTX and syslog ship cleanly into Kibana via Winlogbeat / Filebeat / Elastic Agent, mapped to ECS. Decide per source whether it's worth the ingest cost — ingest budget is a real constraint, and some logs (verbose Application noise) cost more than they return. Priority score (Section 3) applies to log sources too."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"edr-the-deploy-decision-tree","h":"EDR — the deploy decision\ntree","p":"Collection Plan Methodology › 6. Host collection\n— what to request vs deploy","t":"EDR gives real-time process/network/file/registry telemetry, in-memory behavioral detection, and a response capability (isolate/kill/collect). It's powerful and it's intrusive — a kernel-level agent on someone else's production box. Decide deliberately: For each host (or host group): Q1: Is it OT/ICS, embedded, medical, legacy, or an | unsupported/fragile platform? | YES -> DO NOT deploy an agent. Risk to stability/safety | and likely unsupported. Cover it with PASSIVE | NETWORK collection at its segment boundary instead. | NO -> continue. | Q2: Does ROE / ownership / change control permit an agent here, | and is the host in scope? | NO -> request logs only (or passive network). Stop. | YES -> continue. | Q3: Do you need a RESPONSE capability here (isolate, kill, | remote-collect) or real-time BEHAVIORAL / memory detection | (e.g. cred dumping, injection) that logs won't give you? | YES -> DEPLOY EDR. | NO -> continue. | Q4: Is this a high-value / high-likelihood host — DC, jump host, | crown-jewel server, externally exposed, known/suspected | foothold or pivot? | YES -> DEPLOY EDR (start here, expand outward by priority). | NO -> continue. | Q5: Does native logging (EVTX+Sysmon / auditd) already cover the | threat adequately for this host's priority? | YES -> request logs; skip the agent (save it for where it | earns its place). | NO -> DEPLOY EDR. | Overlay (can override a YES): is a COVERT posture required because you're observing a live actor? A new agent install is detectable and can tip them off / trigger anti-forensics. If so, weigh passive + existing-log collection first and stage EDR for the containment phase. When you should NOT deploy EDR — collected in one place: OT/ICS/embedded/medical/legacy or any platform where the agent risks stability or isn't supported -> passive network only. Hosts outside ROE / ownership, or under change control that forbids it. When covertness matters more than depth right now (live actor you're watching) — the install is noisy. When native logging already covers the threat and the host is low priority — don't spend the agent slot. Where it merely duplicates coverage you already have. Deployment order when you do deploy: crown jewels and likely targets -> pivot points (jump hosts, DCs) -> externally exposed servers -> suspected foothold hosts -> expand down the priority list as capacity allows. You rarely blanket-deploy; you deploy by priority, same as everything else in this plan."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"7-assumptions-and-category-markers","h":"7. Assumptions and category\nmarkers","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"Every requirement and every sensor/host decision rests on assumptions, and the assumptions differ sharply between a range and a real client. Tag each one so you know what to revalidate when the environment changes: Marker Meaning Why it matters [RANGE] Holds on the range / exercise network Synthetic traffic, known build, duplicated images, orchestration and traffic-generator noise, no real production impact, decryption often available. Many \"normals\" are range artifacts, not real. [CLIENT] Holds on a real operational network Unknown build, real users, real production-impact risk, strict ROE, legal boundaries, encryption you can't break, change control. Assumptions here are unverified until confirmed on site. [BOTH] Holds regardless of environment Platform/protocol truths (AD uses Kerberos, SMB on 445, DNS on 53). Safe to carry across. Why this is its own section and not a footnote: the single most common way a good plan fails is carrying a range assumption onto a real client (or vice versa). A [RANGE] sensor spot that relied on a known flat build may be wrong on a segmented client network; a [RANGE] \"collect everything, decrypt freely\" assumption is often illegal or impossible at a client. The markers are the hand-off contract between environments — when you move, you walk the list and every [RANGE] / [CLIENT] line gets re-checked; [BOTH] lines you keep. (These same markers feed the baseline doc's pre-site model — a [RANGE] normal is provisional in a way a [BOTH] normal isn't.)"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"8-the-collection-requirement-record","h":"8. The\ncollection-requirement record","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"The atomic unit of the plan. One record per requirement; the fillable worksheet produces these. Field Content ID CR-001, CR-002 ... What The telemetry/data source (e.g. \"PowerShell 4104 script block logs\"). Where Terrain location (segment, host group, sensor point). How Collection method (SPAN at core, Winlogbeat forward, Sysmon deploy, EDR agent, triage pull). Why Technique(s) / engagement it serves — ATT&CK ID(s) or objective. This is the justification you defend to the asset owner. Priority likelihood x detection-value x feasibility (Section 3). Marker [RANGE] / [CLIENT] / [BOTH] . Owner Who executes it (you, host admin, network team). Status planned / requested / collecting / failed / gap. Dependency What it needs first (decryption, agent install, ROE approval, ingest capacity)."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"9-fillable-worksheet","h":"9. Fillable worksheet","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"A reusable worksheet ships alongside this methodology (interactive HTML: add/remove requirement rows, set fields and markers, export to CSV or markdown for the binder). The methodology is the how-to-think ; the worksheet is the fill-in-the-blanks that produces the actual plan for a given network. Use the worksheet on site; keep the filled copy with the engagement binder. ASCII skeleton (what the worksheet captures): COLLECTION PLAN — <network> — <date> — <analyst> Engagement: ____________________ Env: [RANGE]/[CLIENT] Sensors avail: __ TERRAIN SUMMARY Segments: ______ Crown jewels: ______ Egress: ______ Core choke: ______ SENSOR PLACEMENT #1 location: ______ axis: N-S / E-W rationale: ______ decrypt: Y/N #2 (if any): ______ covers blind spot: ______ REQUIREMENTS ID What Where How Why(ATT&CK) Pri Mark Owner Status CR1 _________________________ ___________ ___________ __________ ___ ____ _____ ______ CR2 ..."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"10-coverage-validation-and-gaps","h":"10. Coverage validation and\ngaps","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"The plan isn't done when you've listed requirements — it's done when you've proven what you can't see. Two validation passes: Against ATT&CK — lay your planned collection over the technique set from Step 2. Every technique should map to at least one collected data source. Techniques with none are detection gaps — log them explicitly (\"we are blind to in-memory cred theft on the non-EDR hosts\"). Against terrain — walk the diagram. Every segment, crown jewel, and egress should map to a sensor or host source that sees it. Terrain with none is a visibility gap (\"the OT segment has no host collection and the sensor only sees its boundary\"). A named gap is a decision (\"accepted — out of ROE\") or a to-do (\"request second sensor\"). An unnamed gap is how you get surprised. The gap list is a deliverable in its own right and it feeds straight into the baseline doc: a thing you can't collect is a thing you can't baseline, and both facts belong in the engagement record."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"11-quick-start-checklist","h":"11. Quick-start checklist","p":"Collection Plan Methodology","t":"[ ] Pull terrain: diagram, asset inventory, flows, choke points, existing visibility [ ] Pull/translate threat intel -> ATT&CK techniques (none? use platform default set) [ ] Map techniques -> required data sources (host? wire? both?) [ ] Crosswalk onto terrain -> candidate requirements [ ] Score each: likelihood x detection-value x feasibility [ ] Place sensor #1 (egress by default; core if internal engagement); plan #2 to cover its blind spot [ ] Decide host collection: request existing logs; run the EDR deploy tree per host group [ ] Write requirement records; tag every one [RANGE]/[CLIENT]/[BOTH] [ ] Validate coverage vs ATT&CK and vs terrain; log every gap [ ] Hand the filled worksheet to the binder Defensive use. This methodology is for authorized defensive collection, hunt, and monitoring on networks you are tasked to defend, under ROE. Collection placement, decryption, and agent deployment all have legal and operational boundaries — confirm authority before execution."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"baseline-methodology-what-s-normal","h":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","p":"","t":"Purpose. A repeatable way to look at collected data and decide, defensibly, that a given thing is normal — so it can be filtered out and your attention goes to what's left. The output is filters; the methodology is how you earn each \"normal\" verdict instead of guessing at it. This is the second stage of the pipeline: [ COLLECT ] -> [ BASELINE ] -> [ VISUALIZE ] -> [ DETECT ] You can only baseline what the collection plan gave you, and everything you confirm as normal becomes a filter the dashboards and detections apply. Get baselining wrong in either direction and you either drown in noise (nothing filtered) or filter away the intrusion (too much filtered). The whole method exists to make that call honest."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"1-the-organizing-idea-predict-collect-compare","h":"1. The organizing\nidea: predict, collect, compare","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"Most baselining is reactive — you stare at what you collected and try to explain it after the fact. That's slow, it's biased toward \"looks fine,\" and it gives you no way to notice the thing that should be there and isn't. Flip it to predictive . Before you ever see the data, derive what you expect normal to look like from the network build and known environment behavior. Then on site, compare expected against actual. The difference is the product — matched traffic is pre-cleared, and your attention lands on the delta. PHASE 0: PREDICT PHASE 1: COMPARE PHASE 2: TAG (pre-site) (on-site) (ongoing) build + range + intel actual collected data classify each | | observation, v v lock normals, EXPECTED normal ---- diff ----> reconcile 3 ways emit filters (PROVISIONAL) confirm / gap / unknown The discipline that keeps this safe: a prediction is a hypothesis, not a fact. Pre-site expectations enter the register as provisional and only become confirmed after on-site data matches them and they pass one real sanity check. An expectation never silently filters traffic you haven't actually looked at — otherwise the one actor who happens to mimic an expected service gets filtered straight into your blind spot. (This is expectation bias, and Section 8 is about not falling into it.)"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"2-vocabulary-two-kinds-of-normal","h":"2. Vocabulary — two kinds of\n\"normal\"","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"Precision here prevents the whole method from collapsing into \"stuff I've seen before\": Known-good — normal you can affirmatively justify : it maps to a documented baseline item, a range/range artifact, an expected protocol for that terrain, or sanctioned automation. You can say why it's normal. Only known-good gets filtered. Unknown-benign — traffic that looks harmless but you can't yet justify. It has not earned \"normal.\" It stays visible and goes to triage/hunt until it is either justified (-> known-good) or found hostile. The failure mode is treating unknown-benign as known-good because it's quiet and you're busy. The gates in Section 5 exist precisely to stop that."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"3-phase-0-pre-site-build-the-expected-baseline-provisional","h":"3.\nPhase 0 — Pre-site: build the expected baseline (provisional)","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"Before arrival, derive what normal should be from everything you can know without touching the live network. Output: an expected-normal register (every item marked provisional ) plus an expected sensor-coverage map (what each planned sensor/host source should be seeing if the collection plan is right). Sources for the prediction (most also fed the collection plan — shared inputs): Network diagram + asset inventory -> expected hosts, roles, segments, and the flows that must exist (DC replication, DNS to the resolver, workstations to file servers, egress via the proxy). Service/port matrix + authorized-software list -> expected protocols and ports per segment. Known management/monitoring tooling -> expected admin traffic (WSUS/SCCM, patch, backup, vuln scanner sweeps, monitoring beacons). range build / range docs + traffic-gen (e.g. a synthetic-user generator) configs -> expected synthetic users, orchestration traffic, scoring server/grading traffic, injected \"internet\" (see Section 6). Prior-engagement baselines for the same environment -> start from last time's confirmed normals (re-marked provisional until re-confirmed). Vendor / protocol defaults -> expected chatter (NTP, ARP, NetBIOS, mDNS, DHCP, CRL/OCSP, telemetry). For each expected item, record: the observable (host/flow/protocol/process), why you expect it (which source predicted it), the expected sensor that should see it, a confidence, and the environment marker ( [RANGE] / [CLIENT] / [BOTH] — a [BOTH] prediction is higher-confidence than a [RANGE] one). State = provisional . The act of doing this before arrival is half the value even before you compare: it forces you to actually read the build, and it surfaces expected telemetry you'll then check is arriving (a coverage check you otherwise skip)."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"4-phase-1-on-site-ground-truth-and-3-state-reconciliation","h":"4.\nPhase 1 — On-site: ground truth and 3-state reconciliation","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"Capture the actual collected data, then diff it against the expected baseline. Every observable lands in exactly one of three states: OBSERVED on site? yes no +--------------------+---------------------+ EXPECTED | CONFIRM | GAP / ANOMALY | yes | matches prediction | expected but ABSENT:| | -> sanity-check | coverage gap, down | | once, then promote | service, blocked | | provisional-> | collection, or a | | confirmed | real change. INVEST.| +--------------------+---------------------+ no | UNKNOWN | (n/a) | | not predicted: | | | ism? new normal? | | | threat? -> TRIAGE | | +--------------------+---------------------+ CONFIRM (expected + observed). Run the prediction through one real check the first time (is it actually the WSUS server, actually the traffic-generator user pattern?), then promote provisional -> confirmed . Now it can emit a filter. GAP / ANOMALY (expected + absent). The sleeper value of the whole method. Something you were sure you'd see isn't there. Three causes, all worth knowing: (a) a collection/coverage gap — the sensor or agent isn't seeing what it should, your collection plan has a hole; (b) a down/blocked service — operationally relevant; (c) a real change or incident — something altered the environment. Pure reactive baselining never surfaces this cell, because you can't notice the absence of a thing you weren't expecting. UNKNOWN (observed + unexpected). Not predicted. Route through the tagging gates (Section 5): is it a range-ism? a legitimately new normal you add to the baseline? or a hunt lead? This is where real findings hide. The reconciliation is itself an analytic deliverable — the confirm/gap/unknown breakdown tells the engagement lead the state of visibility and the size of the hunt surface on day one."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"5-phase-2-the-tagging-workflow-earning-normal","h":"5. Phase 2 — The\ntagging workflow (earning \"normal\")","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"For any observation that needs a verdict (every UNKNOWN, and anything new that appears later), run it through escalating gates. The first gate that justifies it wins; if none do, it stays visible and goes to hunt. Order matters — cheapest/most-certain justification first. Observation needing a verdict | GATE 1: Is it a known range ism? (Section 6 catalog) | YES -> known-good [RANGE]. Tag, note the ism, emit filter (scoped to range). | NO -> down. | GATE 2: Does it match a DOCUMENTED baseline item (confirmed register, | prior engagement, asset/service matrix)? | YES -> known-good. Tag, link the register ID, emit filter. | NO -> down. | GATE 3: Is it an EXPECTED protocol/flow for THIS terrain? | (DNS from host to resolver, SMB host->file server, Kerberos to DC, | NTP to time source, the documented egress path) | YES -> known-good, but VERIFY endpoints/direction match terrain | (right source, right destination, right port, right volume). | Confirmed -> emit filter. Mismatch -> treat as UNKNOWN, down. | NO -> down. | GATE 4: Is it sanctioned/benign AUTOMATION? | (patch, backup, vuln scan, SCCM/WSUS, monitoring, scoring server) | YES -> VERIFY it's the real source/schedule/volume, then known-good, | emit filter. | NO -> down. | NO GATE PASSED -> UNKNOWN-BENIGN at best. Do NOT filter. -> keep visible, send to hunt / triage as a lead. Each gate has a verify step for a reason: the gate tells you a benign explanation is plausible ; the verify step confirms the traffic actually is that thing and not an actor wearing its clothes (a \"WSUS\" flow to the wrong host, SMB in the wrong direction, a \"backup\" at the wrong hour). Plausible is not confirmed."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"6-range-ism-catalog-gate-1-content","h":"6. Range-ism catalog (Gate 1\ncontent)","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"Range networks generate traffic that looks strange or alarming but is an artifact of how the range is built. Know these cold so you filter them fast — and so you don't mistake a real technique for one. (Draft list; prune/extend per the specific range build.) Ism What you see How to confirm it's the range, not a threat Range NAT / jump hosts Many internal hosts sourcing from one address; odd gateway hops Matches the documented range NAT/jump layout; address is the known range infra Orchestration traffic Control-plane chatter to a management host (provisioning, config push) Destination is the known orchestration host; regular, structured, from the build docs traffic-generator / synthetic users \"User\" activity that's too regular — same sites, same intervals, 24/7, no human rhythm Matches the traffic-generator config; machine-perfect periodicity; originates from the seeded user hosts Cloned-image duplicates Duplicate hostnames, duplicate MACs, same machine SIDs/GUIDs across hosts Images were cloned from one golden build; expected on a range, alarming on a client INetSim / simulated internet \"Internet\" services all resolving to one internal responder; every domain answers, often same IP DNS/HTTP all funnel to the known simulation host; real internet doesn't behave this uniformly PXE / imaging traffic TFTP/DHCP/PXE bursts, large image pulls at range reset Coincides with range build/reset windows; to/from the imaging server Monitoring beacons Periodic check-ins to a monitoring/collection host Destination is the known monitoring stack; your own tooling Scoring server / grading traffic Regular polling to a scoring host, health checks Destination is the exercise scoring server; part of the exercise, not the scenario Why this is a gate and not just a note: these are the highest-volume false alarms on a range, and every one of them has a real-world lookalike (NAT vs C2 aggregation, traffic-generator vs beaconing, INetSim vs sinkhole, cloned MACs vs spoofing). Clearing them first , by matching the documented build, both drops the noise floor and trains you to verify rather than assume. Every [RANGE] filter is scoped to the range and dropped when you move to a client — this ties directly to the [RANGE] / [CLIENT] / [BOTH] markers from the collection plan."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"7-the-baseline-register","h":"7. The Baseline Register","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"The durable record of every \"normal\" verdict. One row per observable; the state column drives the predict->confirm lifecycle. Column Content ID BL-001 ... Date When recorded / last reviewed Scope Segment / host group / sensor it applies to (filters are scoped, never global-by-accident) Observable The thing: host, flow (src/dst/port/proto), process, domain, JA3, user pattern Class network / host-process / host-auth / dns / tls / automation / user State provisional (predicted, pre-site) -> confirmed (observed + verified) -> retired (no longer valid) Justification Why it's normal: ism name, register link, terrain expectation, automation source. No justification -> it doesn't belong here. Marker [RANGE] / [CLIENT] / [BOTH] Filter The emitted filter (KQL / Suricata suppress / Zeek intel exclusion / Defend event filter) — see Section 9 Analyst Who made the call Review-by When to re-verify (nothing is normal forever) Rules: only confirmed items emit active filters. provisional items are visible-but-flagged, never filtered. A filter always carries its register ID so anyone can trace why traffic is being suppressed back to a justification — a filter with no traceable reason is how intrusions get hidden."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"8-guardrails-not-fooling-yourself","h":"8. Guardrails — not fooling\nyourself","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"The method's power (pre-cleared expectations) is also its danger (you see what you expect). Hard rules: Provisional never filters. A pre-site prediction is a lead to confirm, not a license to suppress. Nothing gets filtered until it's confirmed with a verified justification. Confirm = observed + one real check. Promotion isn't automatic on a match; the first match gets a sanity check against the actual endpoints/volume/timing. Plausible is not confirmed. Every gate's verify step exists because a benign category (WSUS, backup, traffic-generator) is exactly what a smart actor hides inside. Filters are scoped and sourced. Every filter has a scope (segment/host) and a register ID. No global suppressions, no orphan filters. Absence is a finding. The GAP cell is not \"nothing to do\" — expected-but-absent gets investigated like any anomaly. Re-verify on a clock. review-by on every row. A normal from three weeks and one patch cycle ago may be stale."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"9-filter-emission-hand-off-to-the-detection-pack","h":"9. Filter\nemission (hand-off to the Detection pack)","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"A confirmed register item becomes a concrete filter in whatever stack carries that data. The exact syntax lives in the Detection & Filter Pack ; the mapping is: Register class Emits to Form network flow / host / port Kibana saved filter; Zeek intel exclusion KQL NOT (...) ; Zeek script/notice suppression Suricata-visible pattern Suricata suppress / threshold by sig+host, or scoped rule host process / parent-child / user pattern Elastic Defend event filter (drop at agent) or exception (on a rule) DNS / TLS (JA3, SNI) Kibana / Zeek KQL filter; Zeek intel-framework exclusion Baseline filtering is applied first in the dashboards and detections — confirmed-normal drops out before analysis — so the noise floor set here directly determines the signal downstream. This is why the register's discipline matters: a sloppy \"normal\" here silently blinds every dashboard and rule that inherits the filter."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"10-quick-start-checklist","h":"10. Quick-start checklist","p":"Baseline Methodology —\n\"What's Normal\"","t":"PRE-SITE (Phase 0) [ ] Read the build: diagram, assets, service matrix, range/traffic-generator docs, prior baselines [ ] Derive expected normals -> register, every row state=provisional, marked [RANGE]/[CLIENT]/[BOTH] [ ] Build expected sensor-coverage map (what each source SHOULD see) ON-SITE (Phase 1) [ ] Capture actual collected data [ ] 3-state diff vs expected: CONFIRM / GAP-ANOMALY / UNKNOWN [ ] Work the GAP cell: coverage hole? down service? real change? [ ] Promote confirmed (observed + sanity check): provisional -> confirmed ONGOING (Phase 2) [ ] Run every UNKNOWN through Gates 1-4; verify, don't assume [ ] Known-good -> register + scoped, sourced filter; unknown-benign -> stays visible, to hunt [ ] Set review-by on every row; re-verify on schedule [ ] Drop all [RANGE] filters when moving to a client network Defensive use. Baselining determines what gets filtered out of defensive analysis. The guardrails exist so that filtering never hides hostile activity. Applied as written — justify every normal, scope every filter, treat absence as a finding — the method reduces noise without reducing coverage."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"kibana-dashboards-host-network","h":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","p":"","t":"Purpose. Standard dashboards that let you triage a host and a network view fast, from a known-good / known-bad posture. This is the third pipeline stage — the visualize layer that sits on top of what you collected and after baseline filtering removes the confirmed-normal: [ COLLECT ] -> [ BASELINE ] -> [ VISUALIZE ] -> [ DETECT ] A dashboard shows only what collection delivered and baseline left behind. Build these to mirror that pipeline, not as a wall of pretty charts."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"1-organizing-idea-funnel-and-one-question-per-panel","h":"1.\nOrganizing idea: funnel, and one question per panel","p":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","t":"Two rules shape every dashboard here: Each panel answers one hunting question. Not \"show me network traffic\" — \"which internal hosts are beaconing to an external destination on a fixed interval?\" If you can't state the question a panel answers, cut it. A panel with no question is decoration, and decoration is where findings hide. Layout is a funnel: overview -> drilldown. Top of the dashboard is the widest, cheapest triage (counts, top-N, timelines) so you can see the shape of the data in one screen. Lower panels get narrower and more specific. You scan the top, something looks off, you click down. This matches how you actually hunt and it keeps the dashboard usable on a bad-bandwidth day on site. Three dashboards, in funnel order: Overview (landing, both axes) -> Host -> Network . Baseline filters (from the baseline doc's register) are applied as pinned filters so confirmed-normal is already gone before you look."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"2-data-model-assumptions-ecs-indices","h":"2. Data model assumptions\n(ECS + indices)","p":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","t":"Built on Elastic Common Schema (ECS) so host and network fields line up for correlation. Assumed sources/indices (adjust to the stack on site): Source Index pattern / data view Key ECS fields Windows events / Sysmon (Elastic Agent or Winlogbeat) logs-windows.* , winlogbeat-* event.code , process.name , process.command_line , process.parent.name , user.name , winlog.event_data.* , host.name Linux auditd / journald (Elastic Agent) logs-system.* , logs-auditd.* process.* , user.name , event.action , host.name Elastic Defend (endpoint) logs-endpoint.events.* , .alerts-security.* event.category , process.* , destination.ip , dll.* , event.type Zeek (via Filebeat zeek module) logs-zeek.* , filebeat-* zeek.conn.* , zeek.dns.* , zeek.ssl.* , zeek.http.* , source.ip , destination.ip , network.bytes Suricata (eve.json via Filebeat) logs-suricata.* suricata.eve.alert.* , event.severity , source.ip , destination.ip , network.protocol Packetbeat (if used) packetbeat-* network.* , dns.* , tls.* , source/destination.* Mechanics: build data views per source (or one logs-* + the .alerts-* view). Where a field you want isn't mapped, add a runtime field (e.g. parse a domain's length, extract a registry path). Queries below are KQL (Kibana Query Language) unless noted; the Detection pack has the ES|QL / Lucene variants and the Zeek/Suricata rule text."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"3-overview-dashboard-landing","h":"3. Overview dashboard\n(landing)","p":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","t":"Widest triage, both axes, built to be read in one screen before coffee: Panel Question Viz Source/fields Query / notes Normal looks like Pivot on Ingest health Is every expected source still arriving? Area, split by event.module / data_stream.dataset over time all count over time per dataset steady lines per source a line that drops = collection GAP (ties to baseline Phase 1 GAP cell) Alert volume Where are detections firing? Bar, split by signal.rule.name / suricata.eve.alert.signature .alerts-* , suricata last 24h low, known-noise sigs a spike or a new sig Top talkers Who moves the most? Bar, source.ip by sum(network.bytes) zeek.conn exclude baseline internal servers you expect an unexpected host high on the list Egress bytes-out What's leaving? Bar, destination.ip by bytes where destination.ip is external zeek.conn direction = outbound known SaaS/update dests a high-bytes unknown external dest New hosts Anything appear that wasn't predicted? Data table, unique host.name / source.ip not in baseline all compare to Baseline Register nothing new a host = UNKNOWN (baseline Gate it)"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"4-host-dashboard","h":"4. Host dashboard","p":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","t":"Depth on endpoints. Funnel: process activity -> persistence -> identity -> host-network. Each panel is a known hunting technique. Panel Question Viz Source / fields Query (KQL) Normal Pivot Process timeline What's executing, when? Histogram split by process.name Sysmon 1 / 4688 event.code:(\"1\" or \"4688\") steady business apps burst of rare/new names Rare processes What runs almost nowhere? Data table, process.name by count asc, split by host.name Sysmon 1 event.code:\"1\" + sort rare common binaries only a binary on 1-2 hosts Parent-child anomalies Spawns that shouldn't happen Table, process.parent.name -> process.name Sysmon 1 process.parent.name:(\"winword.exe\" or \"excel.exe\" or \"outlook.exe\") and process.name:(\"cmd.exe\" or \"powershell.exe\" or \"wscript.exe\" or \"mshta.exe\") empty any hit (Office spawning a shell) LOLBins Living-off-the-land binaries Table, process.name + process.command_line Sysmon 1 process.name:(\"rundll32.exe\" or \"regsvr32.exe\" or \"mshta.exe\" or \"certutil.exe\" or \"bitsadmin.exe\" or \"wmic.exe\") known admin use odd args (URLs, encoded, temp paths) Encoded PowerShell Obfuscated PS Table, process.command_line Sysmon 1 / 4104 process.name:\"powershell.exe\" and process.command_line:(*enc* or *-e* or *FromBase64* or *IEX* or *DownloadString*) none/known jobs any encoded one-liner Script block content What did PS actually run? Table, powershell.file.script_block_text 4104 event.code:\"4104\" admin scripts obfuscation, download cradles Persistence New autostart Table, event by host.name 7045/4697/4698/4702, Sysmon 13 event.code:(\"7045\" or \"4697\" or \"4698\" or \"4702\") planned installs unscheduled new service/task Logon activity Who authenticated, how? Table, winlog.event_data.LogonType , user.name , source.ip 4624/4625 event.code:(\"4624\" or \"4625\") type 2/3/5 from known hosts type 3/10 spraying, off-hours admin Lateral (explicit creds) Admin reuse across hosts Table, user.name x host.name 4648 event.code:\"4648\" few admin paths one account hitting many hosts Priv use Privilege escalation markers Table 4672 event.code:\"4672\" known admins a workstation user getting priv Host-network by process What is a process talking to? Table, process.name -> destination.ip Sysmon 3 / Defend event.category:\"network\" browser/updater to known lsass/odd proc making external conns EDR alerts Behavioral detections Table, signal.rule.name , host.name .alerts-* all low known-noise malware/injection/ransomware class"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"5-network-dashboard","h":"5. Network dashboard","p":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","t":"Breadth on the wire. Funnel: connection shape -> DNS -> TLS -> HTTP -> alerts. Encryption-aware (metadata when you can't see payload). Panel Question Viz Source / fields Query Normal Pivot Connection overview Shape of all traffic Histogram by network.protocol zeek.conn all expected protocol mix a protocol that shouldn't be there Top talkers / listeners Who dominates? Bars, source.ip / destination.port by bytes+count zeek.conn exclude baseline servers + proxy an unexpected heavy host Egress destinations Where does outbound go? Table, external destination.ip / .as.organization by bytes zeek.conn outbound only CDNs, update, SaaS unknown ASN / raw IP / high bytes Long connections Sessions that never end Table, zeek.conn.duration desc zeek.conn zeek.conn.duration > 3600 backups, DB, RDP sessions long conn to an external unknown Beaconing Fixed-interval check-ins Table/Lens, group source.ip + destination.ip , look at interval regularity of @timestamp zeek.conn see Detection pack (interval analysis) traffic-generator (ism), NTP, update polls regular small conns to one external dst Rare destinations First/seldom-seen externals Table, destination.ip by count asc zeek.conn rare sort none new a brand-new external endpoint DNS volume + NXDOMAIN Resolution anomalies Histogram dns.question.name ; table dns.response_code:\"NXDOMAIN\" zeek.dns all known domains NXDOMAIN spikes (DGA), TXT floods Long / rare domains Tunneling + DGA markers Table, runtime field domain length ; dns.question.type:\"TXT\" count zeek.dns length > 50 or high-entropy normal FQDNs long/high-entropy names, TXT tunneling TLS fingerprints Client/cert anomalies Table, tls.client.ja3 / ja4 , tls.server.x509.issuer , tls.server.ja3s zeek.ssl/x509 all known JA3 set, real CAs rare JA3, self-signed, odd SNI vs cert SNI vs destination Domain fronting / mismatch Table, tls.client.server_name x destination.ip zeek.ssl all SNI matches expected IP/ASN SNI not matching the endpoint HTTP UA + URI Web badness Table, http.user_agent.original , url.path zeek.http all browser/known UAs rare UA, suspicious URIs, webshell paths Suricata alerts Signature hits Table, suricata.eve.alert.signature by event.severity suricata all tuned known-noise high-sev or new category"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"6-build-mechanics","h":"6. Build mechanics","p":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","t":"Data views / index patterns — create per source ( logs-windows.* , logs-zeek.* , logs-suricata.* , .alerts-* ) or a combined logs-* . Set the time field ( @timestamp ). Runtime fields — add for anything not mapped: domain length/entropy, extracted registry path, parsed command-line token, bytes ratio (out/in). Define on the data view so every panel can use them. Saved searches — build the heavy KQL once as a saved search, then base panels on it; keeps queries consistent and editable in one place. Panels — Lens for most (fast), TSVB for time-series/interval work (beaconing), Data Table for triage lists. Give each panel a title that states its question . Pinned / dashboard-level filters — apply the baseline filters here (Section 7) so confirmed-normal is removed dashboard-wide. Pin the environment filter ( [RANGE] range infra out) at the dashboard level. Drilldowns — wire panel drilldowns: click a host.name in Network -> open the Host dashboard filtered to that host; click a destination.ip -> open a destination-detail dashboard. This is the funnel made clickable and it's where triage speed comes from. Time + refresh — default a short relative window (last 24h) with a quick-select; don't auto-refresh aggressively on a constrained sensor. Saved objects / portability — export the dashboards + data views + saved searches as an NDJSON saved-objects bundle so the whole pack imports onto the next stack in one step. Keep that bundle in the engagement binder alongside these docs. Watch index-pattern name drift between environments — re-point data views on import."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"7-baseline-integration-filters-applied-first","h":"7. Baseline\nintegration (filters applied first)","p":"Kibana Dashboards — Host +\nNetwork","t":"The dashboards inherit the Baseline Register. A confirmed register item with a KQL filter becomes a dashboard-level negative filter , so the confirmed-normal is gone before any panel aggregates. Pattern: Baseline Register (confirmed, [RANGE]/[CLIENT]/[BOTH]) | v emit KQL Dashboard-level filters (pinned): NOT (source.ip: <range NAT/jump>) // BL-003 [RANGE] ism NOT (destination.ip: <WSUS> and destination.port: 8530) // BL-011 automation NOT (tls.client.ja3: <known-good JA3 set>) // BL-020 confirmed | v Panels aggregate on what's LEFT = the hunt surface Rules carried from the baseline doc: only confirmed items filter; every filter names its BL-ID ; drop all [RANGE] filters when the dashboards move to a client. A dashboard quietly filtering on an unsourced rule is the same failure as an unsourced register row — the filter list is auditable or it's dangerous. Defensive use. These dashboards visualize defensive telemetry for hunt and triage on authorized networks. The baseline filters reduce noise; keep every filter traceable to a Baseline Register justification so visualization never hides activity."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"intelligence-analysis-methodology-intel-to-action","h":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","p":"","t":"Purpose. Convert a piece of threat intelligence into a defined analytic action: the data to examine, the query to run, and the evidence that confirms or clears it. Intel names a behavior; this methodology resolves that behavior to a record in collected data. It sits between collection and detection and is applied during hunt and triage: [ COLLECT ] -> [ BASELINE ] -> [ VISUALIZE ] -> [ DETECT ] \\ / \\ / -----> [ INTEL -> ACTION ] <----- technique / indicator -> data source -> query -> verdict An intel input is a tasking, not a conclusion. The deliverable of this process is a verdict — present, absent, or not collectable — backed by a query result and a second corroborating source."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"1-the-organizing-idea-every-technique-writes-to-a-known-plac","h":"1.\nThe organizing idea: every technique writes to a known place","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"A technique is an action taken on a host or a network. Every action that matters leaves a record in a specific data source. ATT&CK already names that data source for each technique as a data component — process creation, command execution, network traffic flow, logon session, file modification, Windows registry key modification, and so on. The collection plan already mapped those data components to concrete telemetry: Sysmon, forwarded EVTX, Zeek, Suricata, EDR, memory, and disk artifacts. The intel-to-action path is the composition of those two mappings: TECHNIQUE (T-code) | ATT&CK: technique -> data component(s) v DATA COMPONENT (e.g. \"Command Execution\") | collection plan: data component -> collected telemetry v COLLECTED TELEMETRY (e.g. Sysmon 1 + PS 4104 in Kibana) | this doc: telemetry -> query v QUERY -> RESULT -> VERDICT (+ corroboration) The analyst does not improvise where to look. The technique dictates the data component, the data component dictates the telemetry, and the telemetry dictates the query. When any link is missing, the gap is explicit and named, not discovered by failing to find evidence."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"2-classify-the-intel-input","h":"2. Classify the intel input","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"Intel arrives in five forms. The form sets the entry point into the pipeline. Input Example Entry point Technique ID T1059.001 Section 4 crosswalk — the primary path Tactic only \"credential access reported\" Expand to the technique set for that tactic (Section 7), run each Procedure / tool \"actor uses PsExec\", \"Cobalt Strike\" Resolve the tool to its technique(s), then the crosswalk Atomic indicator hash, IP, domain, filename Section 6 indicator crosswalk Behavioral report \"beaconing to a rare host every 60s\" Describe-to-technique, then the crosswalk Record the input verbatim and its source and date before acting. Stale intel and precise intel are both actionable, but they are weighted differently at the reporting stage. Normalize every input to one or more technique IDs where possible. A tool name is not a tasking until it is resolved to the techniques it performs. PsExec is T1569.002 and T1021.002 . Mimikatz is T1003.001 . The resolution is the first analytic step, and it is recorded."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"3-the-reference-process","h":"3. The reference process","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"Run the same loop for every technique under examination. 1 SCOPE Which hosts/segments could exhibit this technique? Restrict the query to them. 2 MAP Technique -> data component -> collected telemetry (Section 4 table). 3 QUERY Run the mapped query over the incident window. 4 EVALUATE Hit / miss / not-collectable. A miss is only valid if the source was collected. 5 CORROBORATE A hit requires a second independent source before it is a finding. 6 PIVOT A confirmed technique implies adjacent techniques (Section 7). Queue them. 7 RECORD Technique, query, result, corroboration, verdict, confidence (Section 8). Two rules govern the loop: A miss is not a clear unless the source was collected. \"No 4104 events\" means the technique is absent only if Script Block logging was on and forwarded. If it was not, the verdict is not collectable , which is a collection gap, not a negative finding. A hit is a lead until corroborated. One source places a technique; a second independent source confirms it. The corroboration column in Section 4 names the second source for each technique."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"4-technique-to-data-crosswalk","h":"4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"Each entry gives: what the technique writes, where it was collected, the query to run, and the independent source that corroborates a hit. Queries are Kibana KQL against the collected index unless marked. Replace the window and host placeholders. Host-live and memory equivalents are given where the collected telemetry may be absent."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"execution","h":"Execution","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1059.001 — PowerShell Writes: command execution, script block text, process creation. Collected: PowerShell/Operational 4104 and 4103; Sysmon 1; Prefetch POWERSHELL.EXE-*.pf . Query: event.code:4104 and powershell.file.script_block_text:(*FromBase64String* or *IEX* or *Invoke-Expression* or *DownloadString* or *Net.WebClient* or *-enc* or *-ec* or *EncodedCommand*) event.code:1 and process.name:powershell.exe and process.command_line:(*-enc* or *-w hidden* or *-nop* or *bypass*) Corroborate: 4104 script text against the Sysmon 1 command line for the same PID and time; a network child from the same PID. Common benign: SCCM, Intune, admin automation from management hosts — confirm source host against the baseline before clearing. Pivot: parent of the PowerShell process ( T1566 phishing document, T1059 chain), network destination ( T1071 ), files written ( T1105 ). T1059.003 — Windows Command Shell Collected: Sysmon 1; 4688 if process-creation auditing with command line is on. Query: event.code:1 and process.parent.name:(winword.exe or excel.exe or outlook.exe or w3wp.exe or httpd.exe) and process.name:(cmd.exe or powershell.exe) Corroborate: parent process document or service, and any file the child wrote."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"persistence-privilege-escalation","h":"Persistence / Privilege\nEscalation","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1053.005 — Scheduled Task Writes: scheduled job creation, file and registry modification. Collected: Security 4698 create, 4702 update, 4699 delete; Sysmon 1 ( svchost.exe -k netsvcs or taskeng.exe spawning the action); disk C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\ ; registry ...\\TaskCache\\Tree . Query: event.code:(4698 or 4702) and not winlog.event_data.TaskName:(\"\\\\Microsoft\\\\*\") Corroborate: the task XML on disk against the 4698 record; the action binary against a process-creation event at the task's trigger time. Pivot: the action binary ( T1059 , T1105 ), the account that created the task ( T1078 ). T1547.001 — Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder Collected: Sysmon 13 registry value set; Security 4657 if registry auditing is on; disk Run keys and Startup folders; Autoruns output. Query: event.code:13 and registry.path:(*\\\\CurrentVersion\\\\Run* or *\\\\CurrentVersion\\\\RunOnce* or *\\\\CurrentVersion\\\\Explorer\\\\Shell Folders*) Corroborate: the referenced binary against a process-creation event at boot or logon. Pivot: the referenced binary and its signer. T1543.003 — Windows Service Collected: System 7045 service installed; Security 4697; Sysmon 13 under ...\\Services\\ . Query: event.code:7045 and not winlog.event_data.ImagePath:(\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\*\" or \"C:\\\\Program Files*\") Corroborate: the service image path against a process-creation and the on-disk file hash. Pivot: T1569.002 service execution, lateral origin if the service was created remotely."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"defense-evasion","h":"Defense Evasion","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1055 — Process Injection Writes: process access, remote thread creation; the injected region is only in memory. Collected: Sysmon 8 CreateRemoteThread, Sysmon 10 ProcessAccess; memory image. Query: event.code:8 or (event.code:10 and winlog.event_data.TargetImage:(*lsass.exe or *explorer.exe or *svchost.exe)) Memory: windows.malfind for private committed RWX regions; windows.ldrmodules for unlinked modules. Corroborate: Sysmon 8/10 source process against a malfind hit in the same process in the memory image. Pivot: the source process (its origin technique), the target process network and handles. T1070.001 — Clear Windows Event Logs Collected: Security 1102; System 104. Query: event.code:(1102 or 104) Corroborate: a gap in the cleared log's continuity against the 1102/104 timestamp; the account that performed the clear. Note: a log clear is itself the indicator. Treat the window after it as reduced-visibility and lean on memory and disk. T1070.006 — Timestomp Collected: $MFT , USN journal. Query (disk, not Kibana): # MFT: compare $STANDARD_INFO vs $FILE_NAME timestamps per record MFTECmd.exe -f $MFT --csv out ; # then filter records where SI < FN or SI sub-second = .0000000 Corroborate: an execution artifact (Prefetch, Amcache first-seen) that contradicts the file's standard-information time."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"credential-access","h":"Credential Access","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1003.001 — LSASS Memory Collected: Sysmon 10 ProcessAccess to lsass; Security 4656/4663 on lsass if object auditing is on; Sysmon 1 for dump tooling. Query: event.code:10 and winlog.event_data.TargetImage:*lsass.exe and winlog.event_data.GrantedAccess:(\"0x1010\" or \"0x1410\" or \"0x143a\" or \"0x1438\") event.code:1 and process.command_line:(*comsvcs.dll*MiniDump* or *procdump*lsass* or *-ma*lsass*) Corroborate: the accessing process against its origin, and any .dmp file written (Sysmon 11). Pivot: the account whose credentials were exposed, then lateral movement under that account ( T1021 )."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"lateral-movement","h":"Lateral Movement","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1021.001 — Remote Desktop Collected: Security 4624 type 10, 4778/4779; Sysmon 3; TerminalServices-RemoteConnectionManager. Query: event.code:4624 and winlog.event_data.LogonType:10 Corroborate: the source IP against a corresponding outbound RDP connection on the source host; the account against its normal logon hours. T1021.002 — SMB / Windows Admin Shares Collected: Security 4624 type 3, 4648 explicit credentials, 5140/5145 file share access. Query: event.code:4624 and winlog.event_data.LogonType:3 and winlog.event_data.AuthenticationPackageName:NTLM and not user.name:(*$) event.code:5145 and winlog.event_data.ShareName:(*\\\\ADMIN$ or *\\\\C$) Corroborate: one source account reaching many destinations in a short window; a service or scheduled task created at the destination immediately after ( T1569.002 , T1053.005 ). Pivot: fan-out from the source account defines the spread; scope every destination."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"command-and-control","h":"Command and Control","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1071.001 — Web Protocols Collected: Zeek conn , http , ssl ; Suricata; proxy logs. Query: event.dataset:zeek.conn and destination.port:(80 or 443) and not destination.ip:<known_good_cidr> Analysis: group by destination.ip and derive connection interval and byte symmetry. A fixed interval with small, regular request sizes is a beacon. Irregular timing and asymmetric volume is normal browsing. Corroborate: the beaconing host's Sysmon 3 for the owning process; the process against Q1 of the host triage. Pivot: the owning process (its persistence and origin), the destination across all hosts. T1071.004 — DNS Collected: Zeek dns ; EVTX DNS client; firewall DNS. Query: event.dataset:zeek.dns and (dns.question.name:/.{50,}/ or dns.response_code:NXDOMAIN) Analysis: high query volume to one parent domain, long or high-entropy subdomain labels, sustained NXDOMAIN — DNS tunneling or domain generation. Corroborate: the querying host's process holding the resolver socket. T1105 — Ingress Tool Transfer Collected: Zeek files / http ; Sysmon 11 file create plus Sysmon 3 network from the same process. Query: event.code:11 and file.path:(*\\\\Temp\\\\* or *\\\\AppData\\\\* or *\\\\ProgramData\\\\* or *\\\\Public\\\\*) and file.extension:(exe or dll or ps1 or bat or scr) Corroborate: the Sysmon 3 network event that preceded the file write from the same PID; the file hash against intel."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"impact","h":"Impact","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1486 — Data Encrypted for Impact Collected: Sysmon 11 high-rate file creation; Security 4663; Sysmon 1 for shadow-copy deletion. Query: event.code:1 and process.command_line:(*vssadmin*delete*shadows* or *wmic*shadowcopy*delete* or *bcdedit*recoveryenabled*no*) Corroborate: a burst of file writes with a common new extension from one process against the shadow-deletion command from the same host."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"linux","h":"Linux","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action › 4. Technique-to-data\ncrosswalk","t":"T1053.003 — Cron Collected: auditd execve; /etc/cron* , /var/spool/cron/ ; syslog cron facility. Query (host): for f in /etc/cron* /var/spool/cron/crontabs/*; do echo \"== $f\"; cat \"$f\" 2>/dev/null; done Corroborate: the cron command against an auditd execve at the scheduled time. T1543.002 — Systemd Service Collected: auditd; unit files under /etc/systemd , /lib/systemd , ~/.config/systemd ; journald. Query (host): find /etc/systemd /lib/systemd /usr/lib/systemd /home/*/.config/systemd -name '*.service' -mtime -14 -ls 2>/dev/null Corroborate: the unit ExecStart binary against a running process and its on-disk hash. T1505.003 — Web Shell Collected: web-root file creation; web access log; auditd; Zeek http . Query (host): find /var/www -type f -name '*.php*' -mtime -30 -printf '%TY-%Tm-%Td %p\\n' | sort grep -RliE 'eval\\(|base64_decode|system\\(|assert\\(|passthru\\(|\\$_(GET|POST|REQUEST)\\[' /var/www 2>/dev/null Corroborate: a POST in the access log to the suspect path at or after its file-creation time. Pivot: the web process's child shells ( T1059 ) and outbound sockets ( T1071 ). T1098.004 — SSH authorized_keys Collected: file modification; auditd watch on the keys files. Query (host): for f in /root/.ssh/authorized_keys /home/*/.ssh/authorized_keys; do echo \"== $f\"; cat \"$f\" 2>/dev/null; done Corroborate: the key's modification time against an auth.log session for that account."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"5-evaluate-the-result","h":"5. Evaluate the result","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"Three verdicts, and only three. Present. The query returned records consistent with the technique and a second source agrees. This is a finding. Record both sources. Absent. The query returned nothing, the source was confirmed collected for the window, and the baseline for that source is understood. This is a defensible negative. Not collectable. The mapped source was not collected, not forwarded, or not retained for the window. This is a collection gap. It is neither a hit nor a clear. A query that returns records is not automatically a hit. Apply the baseline first — known-good automation, management hosts, and sanctioned tooling are cleared against the baseline register before a record is escalated. Only records that survive the baseline are evaluated against the technique. Record which verdict applies and the evidence for it. \"Absent\" and \"not collectable\" look identical in an empty result set and must never be reported as the same thing."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"6-indicator-crosswalk","h":"6. Indicator crosswalk","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"Atomic indicators resolve to data sources the same way techniques do. Indicator Collected in Query field File hash EDR, Sysmon 1/6/7, Amcache, Prefetch process.hash.sha256 / file.hash.sha256 / Amcache first-seen IP address Zeek conn , firewall, Sysmon 3, proxy destination.ip / source.ip Domain Zeek dns / http / ssl , DNS client, proxy dns.question.name / url.domain / tls.server.name Filename / path $MFT , Sysmon 11, EDR file.path / file.name JA3 / JA4 Zeek ssl , Suricata tls.client.ja3 / tls.client.ja4 Email sender / subject mail gateway gateway index An indicator hit is still one source. A hash seen in Amcache is corroborated by the execution artifact and the network the process generated; a domain in the DNS log is corroborated by the host and process that resolved it. Resolve a hit to the host, then run the host through the technique crosswalk to establish what the indicator did, not only that it was present."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"7-pivot-one-technique-implies-its-neighbors","h":"7. Pivot — one\ntechnique implies its neighbors","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"A confirmed technique is a position in an intrusion, not the whole of it. Each tactic has predecessors and successors. A confirmed technique queues the adjacent techniques for the same host and window. Initial Access -> Execution -> Persistence -> Priv Esc -> Defense Evasion -> Credential Access -> Discovery -> Lateral Movement -> C2 -> Collection -> Exfil -> Impact Worked expansions: T1059.001 PowerShell confirmed -> query the parent ( T1566 , T1203 ), the network child ( T1071 , T1105 ), and any persistence it wrote ( T1053 , T1547 ). T1003.001 LSASS access confirmed -> identify the exposed account, then query that account for T1021.001/002 to the hosts it reached. T1021.002 SMB lateral confirmed -> at each destination, query T1569.002 / T1053.005 for the execution method and restart the loop on that host. T1071.001 beacon confirmed -> resolve the owning process, then query its persistence and the same destination across every host. Pivoting ends when the queued techniques return absent or not-collectable and the timeline is continuous from first activity to last."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"8-confidence-and-reporting","h":"8. Confidence and reporting","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"Two independent judgments, reported separately. Evidentiary confirmation — present, absent, or not collectable, from Section 5. This is about the data. Analytic confidence — how strongly the evidence supports the assessment. Use the standard gradations: almost certainly, highly likely, likely, roughly even chance, unlikely, very unlikely. State the basis — source reliability, corroboration count, collection completeness — and state what would raise or lower it. A technique can be confirmed present with low analytic confidence in attribution, and that distinction is preserved in the report. Evidence of PsExec execution is high-confidence as a technique and says nothing by itself about which actor ran it. Finding record: INTEL IN: source, date, input as received TECHNIQUE: T-code and name; tool/procedure if resolved SCOPE: hosts/segments queried, window (UTC) QUERY: the exact query run, per source RESULT: present / absent / not-collectable, with counts EVIDENCE: primary source + independent corroborating source, each hashed where applicable CONFIDENCE: analytic gradation + basis + what would change it PIVOT: techniques queued from this finding GAPS: sources that were not collectable for this technique"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"9-collection-gaps","h":"9. Collection gaps","p":"Intelligence\nAnalysis Methodology — Intel to Action","t":"A technique whose data source was not collected cannot be confirmed or cleared. Do not report it as absent. Record it as a named gap: the technique, the data component it needs, and the collection that would satisfy it. The gap register feeds back to the collection plan as a requirement — this is the threat-informed input that raises collection above the platform default for the next iteration. GAP: <T-code> requires <data component> ; not collected because <no sensor / not forwarded / retention> ; satisfied by <specific collection> ; priority <by terrain x threat> Reduced visibility is a finding in its own right. An adversary who cleared a log or operated where no sensor sees is still placed by the absence, and the gap is reported, not hidden by an empty query. Defensive and authorized. Applied to collected data from provided systems under rules of engagement."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"detection-filter-pack-suricata-zeek-elastic-defend-kql","h":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","p":"","t":"Purpose. The concrete queries, rules, and filters behind the dashboards — the detect stage, plus the filter emission that the baseline doc hands off to. One reference so the rule text lives in one place instead of scattered across four tools: [ COLLECT ] -> [ BASELINE ] -> [ VISUALIZE ] -> [ DETECT ] | +---- emits filters applied first ----+"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"1-organizing-idea-layers-and-baseline-filters-first","h":"1.\nOrganizing idea: layers, and baseline filters first","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","t":"Detection is layered by vantage point, and the layers cover each other's blind spots (same logic as the collection plan): Network layer — Suricata (signatures, protocol anomalies) + Zeek (behavior, metadata, logs). Breadth, no agent, blind on encryption payload. Host layer — Elastic Defend (behavioral/memory, response) + KQL hunts over EVTX/Sysmon/auditd. Depth, per-host, sees what the wire can't. Baseline filters run before detection, not after. A confirmed-normal register item is suppressed at the earliest point it can be (agent event filter, Suricata suppress, Zeek exclusion, KQL negative). You tune out the known-good so the rules fire on what's left. Every section ends with how that stack emits a baseline filter (Section 7 collects the mapping). Order of operations per layer: baseline filter -> broad hunt query -> specific rule -> alert ."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"2-kql-hunt-library","h":"2. KQL hunt library","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","t":"Run in Kibana Discover / as saved searches / as the basis for detection rules. KQL unless marked ES|QL. These are hunts (wide, for a human) that graduate to rules (narrow, for the alert engine) once tuned."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"host-hunts","h":"Host hunts","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL › 2. KQL hunt library","t":"# Office / email spawning a shell (T1566 / T1059) process.parent.name:(\"winword.exe\" or \"excel.exe\" or \"powerpnt.exe\" or \"outlook.exe\") and process.name:(\"cmd.exe\" or \"powershell.exe\" or \"wscript.exe\" or \"cscript.exe\" or \"mshta.exe\") # Encoded / download-cradle PowerShell (T1059.001 / T1105) process.name:\"powershell.exe\" and process.command_line: (*-enc* or *-EncodedCommand* or *FromBase64String* or *IEX* or *Invoke-Expression* or *DownloadString* or *Net.WebClient* or *-nop* or *-w hidden*) # LOLBins with suspicious args (T1218) process.name:(\"rundll32.exe\" or \"regsvr32.exe\" or \"mshta.exe\" or \"certutil.exe\" or \"bitsadmin.exe\" or \"msiexec.exe\") and process.command_line:(*http* or *\\\\temp\\\\* or *-urlcache* or *javascript:* or *.dll,*) # LSASS access (credential dumping) (T1003.001) — Sysmon 10 / Defend event.code:\"10\" and winlog.event_data.TargetImage:*lsass.exe* and not winlog.event_data.SourceImage:(\"*\\\\MsMpEng.exe\" or \"*\\\\wininit.exe\") # baseline known # New service / scheduled task persistence (T1543.003 / T1053.005) event.code:(\"7045\" or \"4697\" or \"4698\" or \"4702\") # Lateral movement via explicit creds (T1021 / T1078) event.code:\"4648\" # then pivot: one user.name -> many host.name # Failed-logon spray / bruteforce (T1110) event.code:\"4625\" # aggregate by source.ip / user.name, threshold on count # Linux: suspicious execution from web/temp dirs (T1505.003 webshell / T1059.004) host.os.type:\"linux\" and process.executable:(\"/tmp/*\" or \"/dev/shm/*\" or \"/var/www/*\") and process.parent.name:(\"apache2\" or \"httpd\" or \"nginx\" or \"php-fpm\") # web proc spawning shell"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"network-hunts","h":"Network hunts","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL › 2. KQL hunt library","t":"# Beaconing candidates (fixed-interval small conns to one external dst) (T1071) # -> use ES|QL for interval stats: FROM logs-zeek.* | WHERE network.direction == \"outbound\" | STATS conns = COUNT(*), bytes = SUM(network.bytes), uniq_dur = COUNT_DISTINCT(zeek.conn.duration) BY source.ip, destination.ip, destination.port | WHERE conns > 20 AND bytes < 500000 # many small, regular conns | SORT conns DESC # Rare external destinations (first-seen) destination.ip:* and not destination.ip:(<internal ranges>) and not destination.ip:(<baseline externals>) # DNS tunneling / DGA markers (T1071.004 / T1568) zeek.dns.query:* and (dns_qlen > 50 or dns_entropy > 4) # runtime fields zeek.dns.qtype_name:\"TXT\" # aggregate volume by source.ip dns.response_code:\"NXDOMAIN\" # spike by source.ip = DGA # Self-signed / odd TLS (T1573 / C2 over TLS) tls.server.x509.issuer.distinguished_name : tls.server.x509.subject.distinguished_name # self-signed or tls.client.ja3:(<known-bad JA3>) or (tls.client.server_name:* and not destination.ip:<expected for that SNI>) # SNI/IP mismatch # Long-lived external sessions zeek.conn.duration > 3600 and network.direction:\"outbound\""},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"3-suricata","h":"3. Suricata","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","t":"Role. Signature + protocol-anomaly engine on the wire. Strong at known-bad (ET/ETPRO rulesets, IOC sigs, exploit sigs, protocol violations). Weak at stateful/behavioral detection over time (beaconing interval analysis) — push that to Zeek/Kibana. Runs best inline with both directions of flow (watch asymmetric routing from the collection plan). Operate the rulesets, don't hand-write everything. Enable Emerging Threats Open (or ETPRO), enable the categories that match your terrain, disable what you can't see. Tune aggressively — an untuned ruleset on a range range is a wall of false positives. Rule anatomy: action proto src_ip src_port -> dst_ip dst_port (msg:\"...\"; flow:...; content:\"...\"; \\ pcre:\"...\"; classtype:...; sid:1000001; rev:1; metadata:...;) Custom examples (defensive, for terrain-specific detection): # Outbound cleartext to a known-bad / unexpected external on a monitored egress alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:\"CUSTOM Egress to flagged host\"; \\ flow:established,to_server; ip_dst <flagged-ip>; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9000001; rev:1;) # Self-signed TLS to external (metadata) alert tls $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:\"CUSTOM Self-signed cert outbound\"; \\ tls.cert_issuer; content:!\"CN=\"; classtype:protocol-command-decode; sid:9000002; rev:1;) # Suspicious DNS TXT volume (tunneling indicator — pair with Kibana aggregation) alert dns $HOME_NET any -> any 53 (msg:\"CUSTOM DNS TXT query\"; dns.query; \\ dns.opcode:0; content:\"|00 10 00 01|\"; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9000003; rev:1;) # SMB lateral indicator (admin share from a workstation subnet) alert smb $WKSTN_NET any -> $SERVER_NET 445 (msg:\"CUSTOM Wkstn->Server admin share\"; \\ flow:to_server; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9000004; rev:1;) range / baseline suppression — knock down the range-ism noise (Gate 1 of the baseline doc) in threshold.config : # Suppress a sig entirely from the range NAT/jump source (BL-003 [RANGE]) suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2000419, track by_src, ip <range-nat-ip> # Rate-limit a noisy-but-real sig to one alert per 5 min per host threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 9000003, type threshold, track by_src, count 1, seconds 300 # Suppress traffic-generator synthetic-user hosts from a web-category sig (BL-007 [RANGE]) suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2013028, track by_src, ip <traffic-gen-host-range> Every suppress carries its BL-ID in a comment — the suppression is traceable back to a confirmed register item, or it doesn't go in."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"4-zeek","h":"4. Zeek","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","t":"Role. The network's system-of-record and behavioral engine. Where Suricata says \"this packet matched,\" Zeek says \"here is every connection, DNS query, TLS handshake, HTTP request, and file seen, as structured logs you can hunt and baseline.\" Best for the stateful/over-time detection Suricata is weak at, and the richest source for the Network dashboard. Logs to mine (ship to Kibana via Filebeat zeek module): Log What it gives Hunt use conn.log every flow: duration, bytes, state, history beaconing, long conns, top talkers, exfil ratios dns.log queries, responses, rcodes, types DGA, tunneling, NXDOMAIN spikes, TXT volume http.log method, host, URI, UA, status webshell URIs, rare UAs, suspicious downloads ssl.log + x509.log SNI, versions, cert subject/issuer/validity, JA3/JA3S self-signed, rare JA3, SNI/IP mismatch files.log files seen, MIME, hashes malware delivery, carving (with file extraction) notice.log Zeek's own detections pre-built behavioral notices weird.log protocol anomalies tunneling, evasion, malformed traffic JA3/JA4 — load the JA3 package (or built-in JA4 on current Zeek) to fingerprint TLS clients/servers; baseline the known-good JA3 set, alert/flag the rest. Notice framework — turn a hunt into a standing detection. Example (long outbound connection): # long-conns.zeek — notice on outbound sessions over 1h @load base/frameworks/notice redef enum Notice::Type += { Long_Outbound_Conn }; event connection_state_remove(c: connection) { if ( c$duration > 1hr && Site::is_local_addr(c$id$orig_h) && ! Site::is_local_addr(c$id$resp_h) ) NOTICE([$note=Long_Outbound_Conn, $conn=c, $msg=fmt(\"Long outbound conn %s -> %s (%s)\", c$id$orig_h, c$id$resp_h, c$duration)]); } Intel framework — feed IOCs (domains, IPs, hashes, JA3) from threat intel; Zeek matches them across all logs and writes intel.log . This is the clean place to do IOC matching from the collection plan's intel inputs. Baseline exclusion — the Zeek way to filter confirmed-normal is to exclude in the detection script , not to drop the log (keep the record, suppress the alert): # in a notice policy hook — suppress the ism, cite the register ID hook Notice::policy(n: Notice::Info) { if ( n$note == Long_Outbound_Conn && n$conn$id$resp_h == <backup-server> ) break; # BL-014 [BOTH] confirmed: nightly backup, not a finding }"},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"5-elastic-defend-endpoint","h":"5. Elastic Defend (endpoint)","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","t":"Role. The host behavioral + response layer. Real-time process/file/network/registry/DLL events, in-memory and behavioral protections (malware, ransomware, memory-threat, credential-access), and the response actions (isolate host, kill process, remote collect). This is what the collection plan's EDR deploy tree puts on the high-value hosts. Enable: Detection rules — turn on the Elastic prebuilt rule set, filtered to the platforms you've deployed on (Windows/Linux). Start with the \"recommended\" + the ATT&CK coverage matching your threat (from the collection plan's technique list). Don't enable all 1000+ cold on a range — you'll bury yourself. Endpoint protections — malware (ML), ransomware (behavioral), memory-threat, and malicious-behavior, set to detect first on a range/sensitive client, prevent once tuned and authorized. Prevent on a production client without authorization is how you cause an outage. Response — enable host isolation / process kill only where ROE permits; this is a live action on someone's box. Baseline filtering — two mechanisms, know the difference: Mechanism What it does Use for Event filter Stops matching events from being written/ingested at the agent High-volume known-good telemetry you don't want at all (range orchestration, traffic-generator process noise). Cheapest filter, earliest point. Rule exception Stops a specific rule from alerting on a match, event still ingested Known-good that trips one rule but you still want the raw event (admin tool that looks like a LOLBin). Trusted application Excludes a binary/path/signer from protections A known tool the malware/behavior engine flags (a red-team or admin utility you've sanctioned). Example event filter (drop traffic-generator synthetic-user browser noise — baseline Gate 1): # Event filter (BL-007 [RANGE] traffic-generator) event.category : \"process\" and process.parent.name : \"<traffic-gen>.exe\" Example rule exception (admin's legit certutil use trips the LOLBin rule): # Exception on \"Certutil URL download\" rule (BL-022 [CLIENT] confirmed admin host) host.name : \"ADM-TOOLS-01\" and user.name : \"svc-patch\" Rules carried through: event filter for volume you never want (earliest, cheapest); exception when you still want the event; trusted-app only for protection engines. Every one cites its BL-ID ; [RANGE] filters come off at the client. Prefer the event filter for pure range noise (keeps the index clean), the exception when the raw event still has hunt value."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"6-mapping-the-layers-to-the-threat-coverage-check","h":"6. Mapping\nthe layers to the threat (coverage check)","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","t":"Tie the pack back to ATT&CK so you can prove coverage (same validation idea as the collection plan Section 10): ATT&CK (example) Network (Suricata/Zeek) Host (Defend/KQL) T1071 App-layer C2 Zeek conn beaconing, ssl JA3, Suricata C2 sigs Defend network events by process T1071.004 DNS C2 Zeek dns (TXT/NXDOMAIN/entropy), Suricata dns — T1059.001 PowerShell — KQL 4104 / encoded PS, Defend behavioral T1003 Cred dumping — Defend memory-threat, KQL Sysmon 10 LSASS T1021 Lateral movement Zeek smb/conn, Suricata smb sig KQL 4648/4624 type 3/10 T1486 Ransomware Zeek files (mass rename weird) Defend ransomware behavioral T1048 Exfil Zeek conn bytes-out ratio, long conns Defend network by process A technique with no row on either side is a detection gap — log it, same as the collection and baseline docs. Coverage proven across all four docs is the point of building them as one pipeline."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"7-filter-emission-one-table-all-stacks","h":"7. Filter emission —\none table, all stacks","p":"Detection\n& Filter Pack — Suricata / Zeek / Elastic Defend / KQL","t":"The hand-off from the Baseline Register. A confirmed register item becomes: Register class Stack Filter form Earliest point host process / user pattern (range noise) Elastic Defend event filter at agent (not ingested) host process tripping one rule Elastic Defend rule exception at rule (event kept) sanctioned tool flagged by protection Elastic Defend trusted application protection engine network sig false-positive / ism Suricata suppress / threshold by sig+host sensor network behavior known-good Zeek Notice::policy hook break notice engine (log kept) IOC allowlist Zeek intel-framework exclusion intel match any Kibana-visible flow/host/dns/tls Kibana pinned NOT (...) dashboard filter query time Three rules govern all of them, inherited from the baseline doc: Only confirmed items filter. Provisional predictions never suppress. Every filter cites its BL-ID . Traceable to a justification or it doesn't ship. [RANGE] filters are dropped at the client. The environment marker is the switch. Defensive use. Every rule, query, and filter here is for authorized defensive detection and hunt. Prevention/response actions (Suricata inline drop, Defend prevent/isolate) are live actions — enable them only within ROE and after tuning in detect mode. Baseline filters reduce noise, never coverage: keep each one traceable so detection is never silently blinded."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"ics-scada-defensive-overview","h":"ICS/SCADA Defensive Overview","p":"","t":"Orientation for a host/network analyst dropped onto an OT network. Defensive framing: what the systems are, how they talk, where they're weak, and what an attack looks like on the wire and on the host. One rule above all others: on OT, passive first. An active scan that is harmless on IT can hang a PLC or trip a safety function. Do nothing that writes, probes, or floods until the asset owner clears it."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"1-the-systems","h":"1. The Systems","p":"ICS/SCADA Defensive Overview","t":"ICS (Industrial Control System) is the umbrella. SCADA (Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition) is the geographically distributed kind — pipelines, grid, water — where a central master polls remote sites. DCS (Distributed Control System) is the plant-local kind — a refinery or factory, control staying inside the facility. Same components, different spread. Component Role Analyst note PLC Programmable Logic Controller — runs the physical process (valves, motors) on a scan loop The target. Logic lives in its memory; \"program download\" changes the process RTU Remote Terminal Unit — field data collection at a remote SCADA site Like a PLC, built for remote/telemetry links IED Intelligent Electronic Device — protective relay, breaker controller (power) Speaks IEC 61850; trips breakers HMI Human-Machine Interface — operator's screen/controls Usually Windows. Your most likely host-forensics target EWS Engineering Workstation — where logic is written and pushed to PLCs Highest-value host. Owns the keys to the process Historian Time-series database of process values Windows/DB. Bridges OT and IT; common pivot SIS Safety Instrumented System — independent safety shutdown Hands-off. Target of TRITON. Interaction can endanger people Purdue model — the reference segmentation. Know which level you're on; it sets what's normal. L5 Enterprise (corporate IT, internet) L4 Business / plant IT (email, ERP) ---- IT / OT boundary ---- L3.5 Industrial DMZ <- historian replica, jump host, patch/AV live here L3 Site operations (historian, domain, EWS) L2 Supervisory (HMI, SCADA servers) L1 Control (PLC, RTU, IED, SIS) L0 Process (sensors, actuators — the physical world) Traffic that crosses levels it shouldn't — L4 reaching straight to an L1 PLC — is the single most useful anomaly to look for."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"2-the-protocols","h":"2. The Protocols","p":"ICS/SCADA Defensive Overview","t":"OT protocols were built for reliable real-time telemetry on trusted serial loops, then lifted onto TCP/IP. Most carry no authentication and no encryption by design. On a flat or poorly segmented network that is the whole problem: anyone who can route a packet can command a controller. Protocol Port Domain Auth/crypto Watch for Modbus TCP 502/tcp General / manufacturing None Write-single/multiple (FC 5,6,15,16) from an unexpected host; FC 8 diagnostics DNP3 20000/tcp,udp Electric / water SCADA None (Secure Auth rarely on) Operate/write, cold/warm restart, time-change from a non-master EtherNet/IP + CIP 44818/tcp, 2222/udp Manufacturing (Rockwell) None CIP program download, \"forward open\" to controllers, reset services S7comm / S7+ 102/tcp Siemens Weak/none (S7+ obfuscated) PLC STOP, program upload/download, run-state change OPC UA 4840/tcp Modern integration Supports it (often off) Unencrypted sessions, anonymous login OPC DA (DCOM) 135 + dynamic Legacy integration Weak Wide dynamic RPC range = firewalling nightmare IEC 60870-5-104 2404/tcp Electric (Europe/Asia) None C_SC/C_DC control commands, interrogation from unknown source IEC 61850 MMS 102/tcp Substations None Control to IEDs IEC 61850 GOOSE / SV L2 multicast (no IP) Substations, fast trip None Spoofed GOOSE with high stNum to force a trip; won't show in an IP capture BACnet/IP 47808/udp Building automation (HVAC) None Write-property to controllers PROFINET L2 + 34962-34964 Siemens fieldbus None Layer-2; needs a SPAN on the right segment Rule of thumb: these are plaintext . In a pcap you can usually read the command intent directly. That's bad for the defender's network and good for the defender's packet analysis."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"3-common-weaknesses","h":"3. Common Weaknesses","p":"ICS/SCADA Defensive Overview","t":"Ordered roughly by how often they're the finding. Most OT incidents are not zero-days; they're these. Flat networks / weak segmentation. No real boundary between IT and OT, or between Purdue levels. One phished L4 workstation reaches L1. Look for routes and sessions that cross levels. Internet-exposed OT. HMIs, PLCs, and VNC/RDP reachable from the internet (findable on Shodan/Censys). Check for public-facing 502, 44818, 20000, 47808 and remote-access ports. No authentication on control protocols. By design (§2). Compensated only by segmentation — so #1 and #3 compound. Default / shared / hardcoded credentials. Vendor defaults on HMIs, switches, and web UIs; shared operator logins with no attribution. Unpatched, end-of-life systems. Windows 7/XP HMIs, decade-old firmware. Patching is hard because uptime is sacred — so legacy CVEs persist for years. Dual-homed hosts bridging IT and OT. Historians and EWS with a NIC in each world — the designed-in pivot path. Enumerate multi-homed assets. Unmanaged remote access. Vendor maintenance via TeamViewer/VNC/cellular modems, often outside the firewall and unlogged. Removable media & the EWS. USB is how air-gapped plants get updates — and malware (Stuxnet). The EWS is the crown jewel: own it and you own the logic pushed to every PLC. No OT monitoring. Many sites have zero packet visibility below L3. You often can't answer \"is this normal?\" because nobody ever baselined it. Baselining is itself a deliverable."},{"d":"Analyst Methods Handbook","s":"methods-handbook","a":"4-identifying-attacks-manipulation","h":"4. Identifying Attacks /\nManipulation","p":"ICS/SCADA Defensive Overview","t":"OT favors the defender in one way: the process is repetitive and predictable . Normal traffic is the same masters polling the same slaves at the same intervals with the same function codes. Malice shows up as deviation from that rhythm. On the wire New talkers. A host that never spoke a control protocol suddenly issuing Modbus writes or S7 commands. Baseline who-talks-to-whom; flag the new edge. Write/control where you normally see reads. Engineering functions (program download, write-multiple, operate, restart) outside a maintenance window, or from an HMI instead of the EWS. State changes. PLC STOP/RUN transitions, time-sync changes, firmware-update messages you didn't schedule. Scan signatures. Sequential unit-ID / register sweeps, connection attempts across the whole L1 range — recon on a network that should have fixed, stable flows. GOOSE anomalies. Sudden jump in stNum/sqNum, or two publishers for one dataset — a spoof forcing a protective trip. Needs an L2 capture on the station bus. Timing. Poll intervals that change, or a flood that delays real telemetry — availability attacks matter more than confidentiality in OT. On the host (HMI / EWS / historian — usually Windows) Your §1 live-triage and §7–§8 disk/memory flows from the Execution Reference apply. Additions for OT: Engineering-software processes or project files changing outside a maintenance window (TIA Portal, Studio 5000, etc.). New/unknown executables on the EWS — the staging point for logic tampering. USB insertion events on air-gapped hosts (EVTX, registry USBSTOR). Historian queries or exports that don't match normal reporting — data theft or recon. Project-file hashes against a known-good baseline to catch logic changes. Frameworks to anchor findings MITRE ATT&CK for ICS — map each observable to a technique (e.g. Modify Controller Tasking , Unauthorized Command Message , Loss of Availability ). It's the ICS-specific matrix, not Enterprise. NIST SP 800-82 Rev 3 — the authority for OT security architecture and controls. CISA ICS advisories — current vendor/product vulns and active-threat alerts. Named ICS malware — the historical pattern (study what each abused) Stuxnet — USB → EWS → Siemens S7 PLC logic tampering; hid from the HMI. Industroyer / Industroyer2 — speaks grid protocols natively (IEC 101/104, 61850, OPC) to trip breakers. TRITON / TRISIS — targeted the Triconex SIS ; the line that should never be crossed. PIPEDREAM / INCONTROLLER — modular toolkit for Modbus, CODESYS, OPC UA; the first \"reusable\" ICS attack framework. FrostyGoop — Modbus TCP to heating controllers; recent, simple, effective. The lesson across all of them: the access path is ordinary IT (phishing, USB, remote access, a flat network), and the OT-specific payload only fires at the end. Defending the IT→OT boundary and the EWS stops most of the kill chain before it reaches the process."}];
window.SEARCH_DOCS=[{"s": "investigation-runbook", "t": "Two-Host Investigation Runbook"}, {"s": "execution-reference", "t": "Execution Reference"}, {"s": "ftk-runbook", "t": "FTK Runbook"}, {"s": "autopsy-runbook", "t": "Autopsy Runbook"}, {"s": "sysinternals", "t": "Sysinternals Reference"}, {"s": "methods-handbook", "t": "Analyst Methods Handbook"}];
